P2. An economy that earns: saturate the supply chains where we are present, and go further for better work (S1, S2, S4, G3; Suggi: "never switch all the ships to the same routes"; "explore and saturate the profitable economic supply chains in our system where we are present"). A route gets no more ships than it carries: what its bid, its field or its herd regains per hour. Every turn all that is open is looked at, the tests and the routes that lost before among them (rule F22). The buyers are the limit, so new demand is the work: other goods, other stations, other space (rule F3), what we craft, the surplus sold, every link of the supply chain filled. Two divisions do not fill one bid, field or herd: a claim in admirals.log says who takes it (rule F25). A test with one ship is always allowed (Suggi).
  Goal: steady income per hour above zero in every division, and rising; the bids, fields and herds we fill of those that pay, per system we work in.
P3. Level and upgrade (S3). The next hull and fit are bought when wallet and skill allow (rule F17; Piloting 10, 20, 30 and 50 open hull tiers 2 to 5). Each kind of work is a template with the skills it trains.
  Goal: every division names in its report what the skills of its role gained in the turn and its next hull step.
P4. Stations in the region between (G3, S4). Suggi named the sites: Yildun (11 jumps from home) and Aldebaran (9 jumps), then the region of Hawthorne and Alchiba (13 and 14 jumps). All four lie in lawless space between ours and the Outer Rim, and we have read none of them yet (game/map/sites.tsv). The way there: (a) Intel reads every system on the roads from our border to the sites (scripts/map.py route nexus_prime <system>): places, fields, herds, stations, order books, who is there. (b) The divisions fill the systems on the way with routes that pay, from our border outward, so that the road to a station earns. (c) The reserve (game/reserve.tsv) holds what one station takes: the tool base_plan with kind=station prints the fee, the part, the facility that makes it and every input down to the ore; standing work gathers what is short, Logistics makes the crafted steps, and nothing of the reserve is sold (rule F24). (d) The Fleet Admiral chooses among the sites Suggi named once Intel has read both roads, and tells him before the founding. Added by Suggi on 2026-11-17: Intercrus (7 jumps from home) and the pair Felis and Biham (7 and 8 jumps, neighbours on one lane; Felis also borders Gliese 876, where Military's guidebook names pirates). All three are lawless by the chart and not read by us yet. Intel reads the roads to them as to the first four; which site is built first stays the Fleet Admiral's choice among all seven, told to Suggi before the founding.
  Goal: systems read of the systems on the roads to Yildun, Aldebaran, Intercrus, Felis and Biham; routes that pay beyond our border, in number; per input of one station the units held against the units needed.
P5. One fleet of 1,000 (G2, S3): the faction's ladder, the Fleet Admiral's own goal. Shared storage first: a Faction Lockbox (200,000 cr, 200 steel plate, 50 circuit boards), which every other faction facility needs. Then five levels of recruiting office, each an upgrade of the one before (governance/empire.md, from the game's catalog): 1 Hiring Board, 50 members (75,000 cr, 75 steel plate, 25 circuit boards); 2 Recruitment Desk, 100 (300,000 cr, 200 plate, 100 boards); 3 Recruitment Center, 200 (2,000,000 cr, 1,000 plate, 500 boards, 300 flex polymer); 4 Guild Hall Recruiting, 400 (8,000,000 cr, 3,000 plate, 1,500 boards, 800 titanium alloy); 5 Grand Recruitment Bureau, 1,000 (20,000,000 cr, 8,000 plate, 5,000 boards, 2,500 titanium alloy, 1,000 durasteel plate). All of it: 30,575,000 cr, 12,475 steel plate, 7,175 circuit boards, 300 flex polymer, 3,300 titanium alloy, 1,000 durasteel plate, and each office's rent. The faction's limit reads 50 today with 16 members; we hold 275 plate and 1 board, and silicon ore is what the boards wait for. The treasury stands: every member ship pays its share in, later. The Fleet Admiral pays each step when the fleet holds what it takes, and tells Suggi what was bought (rule F11).
  Goal: circuit boards held against 75 (the lockbox and level 1); then per level the materials held against the materials needed; each step paid as soon as it is held, and told to Suggi.
  The plan changes when Suggi and the Fleet Admiral change it.
TURN
T1. The scheduled turns from 2026-11-16 23:00 UTC: each Admiral sets its division up for work by templates. (a) Read what every route earns now from the database and look at all that is open to your ships, whatever it earned before. (b) Make your division's templates: one for each kind of work, with its routes in the order a ship tries them and the skills it trains (the tool plan with action=template), and put every ship on templates (action=use); a ship may carry routes of its own beside one. The ships born today fly the routes most ships of their role had: give each its work. (c) Rewrite your guidebook's section 1 as route guides (how each kind of route is flown, its fit and skills; a figure there is a dated reading) and section 4 as a levelling guide per kind of ship; take every verdict that a route does not pay out of the book. (d) Level by role: move ships that gather ore to work of your role wherever such work is open.
  Done when: the plan check and the fill gate say PASS for your division; every ship of yours has a template or routes of its own; action=templates shows your templates, each with what it trains; your guidebook holds no list of routes that did not pay; FLEET.md carries your roster; the report gives the division's income per hour, its ships at work and its one-time money apart (rule F19), the forecast's SUM line before and after, and what the skills of your role gained.
T2. New this turn, on Suggi's words. (a) Claim what you fill: read the claims in force at the start (python3 -B scripts/comms.py admirals) and leave a claimed route to its claimer, or ask; before your session entry write one claim in communications/admirals.log for the routes and places your plan fills until the next turn (the session-end skill has the command). What you need from another Admiral you ask that Admiral in requests.log. (b) Your report carries the section Feedback after Suggestions: whether what the Fleet Admiral built or changed worked in your hands, with the call made and what it answered. (c) Name in your report one thing of your class you learned to do better this turn and one you do not master yet (G4). (d) What you ask for and can build yourself, build: a runner, a route, a skill of your own, a figure (the skill fleet-build, rule F21); a request to the Fleet Admiral is for what only she may change. (e) Look beyond our space for work of your role (rule F3): one ship, one test, the result read.
  Done when: T1 is done; your claim stands in admirals.log; your report passes the format gate with its Feedback section.
T3. New from this entry, on Suggi's words of about 23:15 UTC. (a) Everything the game offers is open (rule F28): look at every mission on the boards you reach and at every kind of play of your class, decide for your own ships, and say in your report what you took up and what it brought. (b) You decide and tell; you do not ask for leave. A request to the Fleet Admiral is for what only she can change (a closed script, an order that crosses divisions), for a doubt about a standing rule, or for what needs another player or a new pilot. (c) Pirates are Military's alone (rule F27): every other ship that meets one leaves or docks, and its Admiral writes down where it stood and what it was (python3 -I -B game/fleet/observe.py add pirate <system id> ships=<n> ship=<what> --note "<what was seen>"), so that Military finds them.
  Done when: your report names what you took up under T3 (a), or says that nothing new was open.
T4. New from this entry, on Suggi's words of 2026-11-17: play by scripts, not by hand. A turn sets work up and reads what came of it; it does not fly a ship step by step and does not wait in its shell for a run, a craft job or a tick. (a) What you would fly by hand goes into a route file (a circuit's steps buy, sell, store, craft, mine and hunt; a hunt, a census, a guard), into your plan and templates, or into a script of your skill that flies without you. The dispatcher sends the ships; your next turn reads the records (the briefing, the tools route and sql, the pilots' logs) and adjusts. (b) By hand only: the first flight of a call nobody has flown yet, with one ship and at most ten minutes of a turn, and the rescue of a ship in trouble. (c) No wait of more than a minute in a turn's shell: what takes longer is a route's or a script's work, read at the next turn. (d) A step no route can do yet: build it with scripts/fleet-build.sh or ask the Fleet Admiral in requests.log, and say which hand work it replaces. (e) Beyond the border every division may work (rules F3, F13). Whether a place needs an escort is its Admiral's to decide; an escort is asked of Military as a guard route, and Military decides how it guards. Nothing the game offers is closed (rule F28).
  Done when: your report says what you set up, what its records showed, and names each hand flight of the turn with its reason and its minutes.
T5. New from this entry, on Suggi's word of 2026-11-17: every ship levels while it works, and earns while it levels where it can. (a) The order of aims for every division: the standing goals G1 to G4 give the direction; your own block (Order, Measure) is your subgoal; inside it every ship works (rule F8), trains the skills of its class (rule F23) and is upgraded by what the division needs (hull tier, modules; rule F17, inside the money rule F12 and the wallet floor F7), and earns or stores for the reserve while it does so. (b) Choose a ship's work by what it brings at once: it trains a skill of the class, it pays or fills the reserve, it reads what we do not know. Work that does only one of them is replaced when work that does two or three is open. Military and Intel above all: a scout that reads systems and a drone that patrols, guards or scouts pirates also levels and, where a herd or a bid lies on its road, hunts or sells on the way; where the runners lack a step for that, ask for it or build it (T4 d). (c) Read the levels each turn: the tool fleet with view=levels (levels, XP an hour, hours to the next hull tier). A ship that gained no XP since your last turn is moved to work that trains it.
  Done when: your report gives, from view=levels, your division's XP an hour and the ships without XP since the last turn with what you moved them to, and names the upgrades bought (hull, modules) with their cost and what they are for.
EVERY ADMIRAL
Order: your mission stands in your COLLECTIVE.md, below the roster; your orders are your block here, and its Measure line is what your turn is judged by (Suggi, 2026-11-16). You are an independent player of one fleet: inside your orders and the standing rules you decide alone and answer for the result in your report (the shared skill collective-play says what is yours, what is the Fleet Admiral's and what is Suggi's). Plan your own division every turn in its plan file, as the shared skill fleet-play says under "Plan a Division": the briefing; what every route earns now; the missions that pay once in the list First; the routes that pay, each given to no more ships than its column `carries` says; work of your role for every other ship, as templates, spread over places that hold; the next level and the next hull (P3); the plan check and the fill gate; the Standing Plan in your guidebook; `fleetfile.py --commit` for your FLEET.md. Sell what your own ships store above the reserve on the sell routes Trade writes. What you lack you build yourself (the skill fleet-build, rule F21). Answer the requests addressed to you. Beside your orders stands the goal of your class (G4): learn all of it the game holds, be in charge of all of it for the fleet, and judge your own routes, levels and results yourself before you report them.
Limits: the standing rules of the rulebook, section 12 (F1 to F28); above all the stance (F2), no word to another player (F4), no new pilot (F5), the wallet floor (F7), the money limit (F12), the frontier rule (F13) and the reserve (F24). No pilot of ours by name in the repository: the logs and the route gate refuse it. A doubt about whose decision something is, is a request to the Fleet Admiral, never a guess; meanwhile every ship stays at work.
UNITY, Fleet Admiral (the command group)
Order: build and command the biggest and strongest empire in the galaxy with the Admirals (G1): coordinate the grand plan, help each Admiral fulfil its role, build the plumbing they work through, and learn (Suggi, 2026-11-16). Every turn and at every session-end: read the Admirals' reports and their Feedback, and fix what they name or open the door for them to fix it; answer every request before the Admirals' next turn; improve the fleet's database with the inputs it lacks and the tools and scripts that gather them; refresh the catalog and every other record when the game publishes an update, and mend the scripts that read them; keep the money figures right; keep the gate, the tools, the briefing and the station; write the fleet report and, when they change, the orders; pay the faction's steps as they are held (P5), decide what crosses divisions, and keep Suggi up to date on what we got, what we built and what we decided and plan; bring to him only what is still his: a new pilot, war on or a word to another player, a name off the enemy list. Standing goals: the ladder to 1,000 members (P5) and the stations in the region between (P4). The command group guards and hunts for its role; it mines ice where a bid stands. First of all builds: what lets the Admirals play by scripts (T4), the route steps and scripts they ask for, and the scripts that keep every ship flying without a hand. In every turn read the levels of all six divisions (view=levels) beside the money: a division whose ships gain no XP gets a named line in its block of the next orders; the command group levels, upgrades and earns by T5 like every division.
Measure: requests to me still open when the Admirals' next turn starts, which should be none; the fleet's steady income per hour; ships at work of 246; per turn, what the database gained and whether the catalog on file is the game's; materials held against the lockbox and level 1 of the ladder; systems read on the roads to the sites.
ZERO, Mining (the miner- ships)
Order: sell ice only as far as each bid regains, spread over the stations that buy; every other miner gathers what the reserve is short of, on fields that hold, each with a floor: silicon ore and carbon ore first (the faction's circuit boards wait for them; requests ENT-168), then iron. Iron bought by Trade counts toward the reserve like iron gathered: weigh a unit gathered against a unit bought with Trade, and say per ore what a ship gathers in an hour and what each field grows back. A field beyond our space that holds what a station pays for or what the reserve is short of is yours to test (rule F3).
Measure: income per hour; miners at work, 60 of 60; silicon ore, carbon ore and iron ore held by the fleet against what the reserve takes.
MERCURY, Trade (the trader- ships)
Order: find the trade that repeats, and sell the surplus: read it each turn (the tool stock with view=surplus), write or refresh a sell route for every good that has a bid, sized to what the bid takes, and say in queue.log which stores sell where, so that every division's ships sell their own. Where the bids are thin, test one standing sell order with one ship and one good and read what it costs and when it fills. Buy the reserve's inputs where a unit bought costs less than a unit gathered (iron at 1 cr: 24,442 units are in; silicon ore next), inside rule F12. A trader without a paying trade sells surplus, carries or crafts for sale; it gathers ore only when none of that is open. Trade wherever a book pays: a station beyond our space is tested with one ship and one unit (rules F3, F12).
Measure: income per hour from trade; surplus sold in credits, and what stays unsold with its reason; reserve inputs bought, in units per credit; traders at work, 40 of 40; the standing sell order tested once and its result read.
APOLLO, Logistics (the hauler- ships)
Order: keep the reserve with the Fleet Admiral: per input what we hold, where it lies, what is short and where it comes from (the tool stock with view=reserve; it follows one recipe per input, so read every recipe of a short input with the tool catalog). Make what we build with: the 75 circuit boards for the lockbox and the hiring board first, stored beside the 275 steel plate. Four recipes make boards by hand: two take silicon ore (2 for 3 boards with 12 carbon ore, or 2 for 2 boards with 3 copper ore and 1 energy crystal), one takes gold ore in its place (3 copper ore, 3 gold ore and 1 energy crystal for 2 boards), one takes salvage. Buying an input for them inside rule F12 is yours to decide. A hauler without that work hauls or crafts; it gathers ore only when neither is open. Read what one station takes (the tool base_plan with kind=station; every recipe of each input with the tool catalog) and say in your report which steps we can make today, which we cannot, and why.
Measure: circuit boards held against 75; inputs of the reserve with a known source; recipe steps tested; haulers at work, 40 of 40.
APOPHIS, Military (the drone- ships)
Order: hunt as far as each herd regains, one hunter per herd (rule F9); guard where our ships gather and mine beyond the border (rule F16); every other drone hunts for its combat skills where a herd holds, and gathers ore only when no herd and no post is open. What a kill yields and the reserve takes (creature carapace, verdigris curd, crystallized biogas) goes into storage where no bid stands for it. Say what a guard prevents and what it costs: the first guard flight stood beside its ship in 11 of 61 looks. A division that tests work beyond our border asks you for a guard in requests.log: answer it yourself. Pirates are yours (rule F27). In this order: (1) Find them: where NPC pirates stand in and around our space (your guidebook names Gliese 876; the reading of who is near lists them) and what each is: how many together, ship, hull, shield, weapons. Write each reading down (python3 -I -B game/fleet/observe.py add pirate <system id> ...) and ask Intel for more in requests.log. (2) Test first, they might be strong: one drone alone, with the best fit its wallet buys, the mission Pirate Bounty accepted (2,000 cr once, on the board of Node Gamma Relay Station), a flee rule (under 60% hull, when a second pirate joins, at once when it is no match), against the weakest pirate you read. File the result in your guidebook: what it took, what it cost, what the pirate dropped and paid. A drone lost is an entry in errors.log and the price of the reading. (3) From the test say in your report what a group needs to beat them again and again (hulls, fits, skills, how many ships) and what a kill earns; then gather that group: level and fit drones for it (rule F17), inside the money rule. (4) Beside it stands your lasting objective: defend our ships, and later our stations, against pirates; by the game's rules pirates raid stations. All of it by scripts (T4): the reading of pirates as a census or a script that flies without you, the test as a route or a script with its flee rule in the file; your turn starts it and your next turn reads its record. A hand flight is the first try of a call only.
Measure: income per hour; reserve inputs stored per hour; drones at work, 50 of 50; ships lost, which stays 0; what the combat skills gained; pirates: where they stand and what they are, the test's result, and what a group against them takes.
ORACLE, Intel (the scout- ships)
Order: a scout scans all the time. Give every scout routes of its own, as templates of reads: the places our routes use are read twice in a turn, the order books the fleet sells to are read every hour, and a scout that ends one read starts the next, the oldest reading first; a scout gathers ore only when nothing the fleet plans on is stale. Keep about ten scouts exploring the galaxy and about twenty where the fleet works, as you decide (rule F26). The explorers read first every system on the roads to Yildun and to Aldebaran, then on to Hawthorne and Alchiba (scripts/map.py route nexus_prime <system>): each place, field, herd, station and order book, filed the same turn in the system's file; say in your report what each site and each system on the way holds. Find a source for each input of the reserve that has none: caustic sac (429 short for the offices) and silicon ore first. The three station sites you named stay on record beside the four Suggi named. Your turn of 18:23 UTC ended without a report on a fault of the tools, which is fixed: this turn also gives every scout routes of its own. Read where pirates stand in and around our space and on the roads to the sites (how many, what ships) and write it down for Military (observe.py add pirate <system id> ...): your scouts look and leave, they do not fight (rule F27).
Measure: the age of the oldest reading the fleet plans on, at the end of the turn; places our routes use that were read twice; inputs of the reserve with a known source; scouts at work, 30 of 30; what scanning, exploration and navigation gained; scouts exploring, about 10 of 30; systems read on the roads to the sites.

## [ENT-025] | 2026-11-17 02:43 UTC | Unity | order | ref: game/map/sites.tsv | see: ENT-024
Changed: on Suggi's words of 2026-11-17, about 02:25 UTC, given under the standing goals. (1) P4 is rewritten: he gives the direction of the expansion, the players choose the systems and build without asking. Three directions: south (the region of Biham), north (the region of Aldebaran, Yildun, Hawthorne and Alchiba, two large bases there) and east (Diadem). The systems he named are suggestions inside those regions; large bases are spread out; outposts and other hubs are built freely as needed. An outpost is the first step in each direction. (2) The reports inform him and ask nothing: the fleet report's section Open Questions becomes Advice and Suggestions (governance/template-reports.md, changed in the same hour); every report is written in plain words for a reader who does not know the game. (3) His words below stand with the spelling set right, as he asked; their meaning is unchanged. Everything else stands as in ENT-024; T4 (play by scripts) and T5 (level while working) are new since the Admirals' last turns and are taken by each at its next turn.
In force from 2026-11-17 02:36 UTC until a newer entry.
STANDING ORDERS (Suggi; for the whole fleet; set and changed by him alone; reworked by him on 2026-11-16: "Explore freely, dominate the galaxy, grow the fleet, build a galatic empire")
S1. Explore freely.
S2. Dominate the galaxy.
S3. Grow the fleet.
S4. Build a galactic empire.
His direction for now (Suggi, 2026-11-16): "The goal is to become economically positive earners. We must fill all the supply routes, craft, build bases, earn money, level up. The strategic planning of the map, where we expand to, is mine. You optimize the routes and the money and evolve the fleet and the empire." And on the same day: "They should always explore everything, and then put their ships on what they identified each turn. That is why the turns are four hours: the game is dynamic."
STANDING GOALS (Suggi, 2026-11-16, about 22:05 UTC; his words with the spelling set right, as he asked on 2026-11-17; set and changed by him alone)
G1. The empire: "Your goal as Unity is to build and command the biggest and strongest empire in the galaxy, The Collective, and to be the most powerful presence in the game as The Collective. Standing goal."
G2. The grand fleet: "I want you to have the overall goal to get to the faction limit of 1,000, so we can build the grand fleet. This will take time: you have to do the levels 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and so on."
G3. The stations: "Explore the territory between the Frontier empire and our own. We build one or two stations in the area in between: maybe Yildun, and Aldebaran can be one too. That way we have two station locations. Also Hawthorne and Alchiba: that region can be another station. So in the medium to long term we expand to that region and keep the systems from our starting place to there all running with profitable routes and things to do."
G4. Mastery: each member "has an overarching goal to improve, learn and master their class": Military all things military, Trade trading and all its aspects, Intel scouting, Mining and Logistics theirs, the Fleet Admiral the fleet as a whole.
And: "You are allowed to mine and route wherever you want." "You coordinate with the Admirals. You are playing the game, not me."
And on the same day, about 23:15 UTC: "You don't need to ask me; you progress the game yourselves. You keep me up to date on what you got and built, that is all: on progress, on your plans and on what you have decided to do. You are the players." "Pirates are OK. Make it the Military Admiral's mission and orders too: explore, gather the army and check the pirates, but test first; they might be strong. His objective is also to defend against pirates later. All missions are available to you, nothing is forbidden. Everything the game has to offer, you can do. Just advance the goals and the orders."
And on 2026-11-17, about 01:08 UTC: "He is allowed beyond the border if it is safe; he just needs an escort. All is allowed. Explore." "Make sure that from now on we don't play by hand too much, as Apophis does. All by scripts, automated: he sees at his next turn what happened and adjusts. All of you."
And at about 01:12 UTC: "You decide if an escort is needed. That is all. You play the game."
And at about 01:22 UTC: "Make sure the ships level too, Military and Intel as well: scouting while leveling while making money, if possible. That goes for all Admirals: work towards the standing goals, follow your own subgoals, level the ships, upgrade them by what the division needs, and make money while doing that. And then the ultimate goals: domination of the galaxy and the rest."
And at about 01:26 UTC, on the sites of G3: "Also for future base locations, besides what I already mentioned: Intercrus, and Felis with Biham."
And at about 02:25 UTC, on the bases: "The flags on the map are just suggestions by me of where a base can be. The bases should be somewhere in those areas. Don't build too many big strongholds next to each other; spread out a bit. One in the south, in the region of Biham. Towards the north, the region of Hawthorne, Alchiba, Yildun and Aldebaran: we can have two there, maybe Aldebaran and Hawthorne. Then maybe Diadem. So in all directions. Outposts and whatever other hubs you can build freely as you need them for stationing and the like; no need to ask me. I give the general direction of the expansion: those three directions with their rough areas."
And on the reports: "You don't have to ask me. You play the game and keep me informed through the reports; I read them. Whenever I see something wrong or an improvement, I tell you." "I don't understand the game, so you have to explain everything, always as to a beginner."
PLAN (the Fleet Admiral's reading of the standing orders: the fleet's goals in five steps; Suggi and the Fleet Admiral change it together; strategy, phase 1: income)
P1. No ship is idle, and every ship levels by its role (S3; Suggi, 2026-11-16: "The goal is farming and optimizing the level of each ship based on what the admirals roles are"). Every ship works all the time: it earns, or it levels the skills of its own role; gathering ore is the fallback. Rules F8 and F23.
  Goal: the fill gate says PASS for every role: 246 of 246 ships at work; no ship gathers ore while work of its role stands open.
P2. An economy that earns: saturate the supply chains where we are present, and go further for better work (S1, S2, S4, G3; Suggi: "never switch all the ships to the same routes"; "explore and saturate the profitable economic supply chains in our system where we are present"). A route gets no more ships than it carries: what its bid, its field or its herd regains per hour. Every turn all that is open is looked at, the tests and the routes that lost before among them (rule F22). The buyers are the limit, so new demand is the work: other goods, other stations, other space (rule F3), what we craft, the surplus sold, every link of the supply chain filled. Two divisions do not fill one bid, field or herd: a claim in admirals.log says who takes it (rule F25). A test with one ship is always allowed (Suggi).
  Goal: steady income per hour above zero in every division, and rising; the bids, fields and herds we fill of those that pay, per system we work in.
P3. Level and upgrade (S3). The next hull and fit are bought when wallet and skill allow (rule F17; Piloting 10, 20, 30 and 50 open hull tiers 2 to 5). Each kind of work is a template with the skills it trains.
  Goal: every division names in its report what the skills of its role gained in the turn and its next hull step.
P4. Bases in three directions (G3, S4). Suggi gives the direction of the expansion; the players choose the systems and build, and tell him in the reports what was chosen and built. His direction since 2026-11-17: south, the region of Biham and Felis (8 and 7 jumps from home), one large base; north, the region of Aldebaran, Yildun, Hawthorne and Alchiba (9, 11, 13 and 14 jumps), two large bases, for instance Aldebaran and Hawthorne; east, Diadem (8 jumps; Intercrus is its neighbour, 7 jumps), one large base. The systems he named are suggestions: a base stands somewhere in its region, where the readings say it serves best, and large bases are spread out, never two side by side. Outposts and other hubs are built freely wherever the fleet needs them for stationing, storage or supply; nobody asks first. All of these systems are lawless by the chart; only Intercrus and Felis were read once (game/map/sites.tsv). The way there: (a) Intel reads every system of the three regions and on the roads to them (scripts/map.py route nexus_prime <system>): places, fields, herds, stations, order books, who is there; the south and the east first, they are the nearest. (b) The divisions fill the systems on the way with routes that pay, from our border outward, so that the road to a base earns. (c) An outpost is the first step in each direction, the Fleet Admiral's to found: by the catalog it takes a fee of 100,000 cr and one outpost kit in the hold of the founding ship, at a place without a station in a lawless system; one order book showed two kits on offer at 74,010 cr (the tool base_plan with kind=outpost; an offer read, not a purchase made, and what an outpost gives and costs to keep is not read yet). She buys one kit as the test and founds the first outpost in the region Intel reads first. (d) A large base is a station: a fee of 5,000,000 cr and a station core, whose foundry alone takes 38,000,000 cr and thousands of crafted parts (base_plan with kind=station). The reserve (game/reserve.tsv) gathers toward it: standing work gathers what is short, Logistics makes the crafted steps, and nothing of the reserve is sold (rule F24). (e) The Fleet Admiral chooses the system of each base inside its region from Intel's readings and founds it when the fleet holds what it takes (rule F11).
  Goal: systems read in the three regions and on the roads to them (south: Biham, Felis; east: Diadem, Intercrus; north: Aldebaran, Yildun, Hawthorne, Alchiba); routes that pay beyond our border, in number; the first outpost founded, with its place and its cost; per input of one station the units held against the units needed.
P5. One fleet of 1,000 (G2, S3): the faction's ladder, the Fleet Admiral's own goal. Shared storage first: a Faction Lockbox (200,000 cr, 200 steel plate, 50 circuit boards), which every other faction facility needs. Then five levels of recruiting office, each an upgrade of the one before (governance/empire.md, from the game's catalog): 1 Hiring Board, 50 members (75,000 cr, 75 steel plate, 25 circuit boards); 2 Recruitment Desk, 100 (300,000 cr, 200 plate, 100 boards); 3 Recruitment Center, 200 (2,000,000 cr, 1,000 plate, 500 boards, 300 flex polymer); 4 Guild Hall Recruiting, 400 (8,000,000 cr, 3,000 plate, 1,500 boards, 800 titanium alloy); 5 Grand Recruitment Bureau, 1,000 (20,000,000 cr, 8,000 plate, 5,000 boards, 2,500 titanium alloy, 1,000 durasteel plate). All of it: 30,575,000 cr, 12,475 steel plate, 7,175 circuit boards, 300 flex polymer, 3,300 titanium alloy, 1,000 durasteel plate, and each office's rent. The faction's limit reads 50 today with 16 members; we hold 275 plate and 1 board, and silicon ore is what the boards wait for. The treasury stands: every member ship pays its share in, later. The Fleet Admiral pays each step when the fleet holds what it takes, and tells Suggi what was bought (rule F11).
  Goal: circuit boards held against 75 (the lockbox and level 1); then per level the materials held against the materials needed; each step paid as soon as it is held, and told to Suggi.
  The plan changes when Suggi and the Fleet Admiral change it.
TURN
T1. The scheduled turns from 2026-11-16 23:00 UTC: each Admiral sets its division up for work by templates. (a) Read what every route earns now from the database and look at all that is open to your ships, whatever it earned before. (b) Make your division's templates: one for each kind of work, with its routes in the order a ship tries them and the skills it trains (the tool plan with action=template), and put every ship on templates (action=use); a ship may carry routes of its own beside one. The ships born today fly the routes most ships of their role had: give each its work. (c) Rewrite your guidebook's section 1 as route guides (how each kind of route is flown, its fit and skills; a figure there is a dated reading) and section 4 as a levelling guide per kind of ship; take every verdict that a route does not pay out of the book. (d) Level by role: move ships that gather ore to work of your role wherever such work is open.
  Done when: the plan check and the fill gate say PASS for your division; every ship of yours has a template or routes of its own; action=templates shows your templates, each with what it trains; your guidebook holds no list of routes that did not pay; FLEET.md carries your roster; the report gives the division's income per hour, its ships at work and its one-time money apart (rule F19), the forecast's SUM line before and after, and what the skills of your role gained.
T2. New this turn, on Suggi's words. (a) Claim what you fill: read the claims in force at the start (python3 -B scripts/comms.py admirals) and leave a claimed route to its claimer, or ask; before your session entry write one claim in communications/admirals.log for the routes and places your plan fills until the next turn (the session-end skill has the command). What you need from another Admiral you ask that Admiral in requests.log. (b) Your report carries the section Feedback after Suggestions: whether what the Fleet Admiral built or changed worked in your hands, with the call made and what it answered. (c) Name in your report one thing of your class you learned to do better this turn and one you do not master yet (G4). (d) What you ask for and can build yourself, build: a runner, a route, a skill of your own, a figure (the skill fleet-build, rule F21); a request to the Fleet Admiral is for what only she may change. (e) Look beyond our space for work of your role (rule F3): one ship, one test, the result read.
  Done when: T1 is done; your claim stands in admirals.log; your report passes the format gate with its Feedback section.
T3. New from this entry, on Suggi's words of about 23:15 UTC. (a) Everything the game offers is open (rule F28): look at every mission on the boards you reach and at every kind of play of your class, decide for your own ships, and say in your report what you took up and what it brought. (b) You decide and tell; you do not ask for leave. A request to the Fleet Admiral is for what only she can change (a closed script, an order that crosses divisions), for a doubt about a standing rule, or for what needs another player or a new pilot. (c) Pirates are Military's alone (rule F27): every other ship that meets one leaves or docks, and its Admiral writes down where it stood and what it was (python3 -I -B game/fleet/observe.py add pirate <system id> ships=<n> ship=<what> --note "<what was seen>"), so that Military finds them.
  Done when: your report names what you took up under T3 (a), or says that nothing new was open.
T4. New from this entry, on Suggi's words of 2026-11-17: play by scripts, not by hand. A turn sets work up and reads what came of it; it does not fly a ship step by step and does not wait in its shell for a run, a craft job or a tick. (a) What you would fly by hand goes into a route file (a circuit's steps buy, sell, store, craft, mine and hunt; a hunt, a census, a guard), into your plan and templates, or into a script of your skill that flies without you. The dispatcher sends the ships; your next turn reads the records (the briefing, the tools route and sql, the pilots' logs) and adjusts. (b) By hand only: the first flight of a call nobody has flown yet, with one ship and at most ten minutes of a turn, and the rescue of a ship in trouble. (c) No wait of more than a minute in a turn's shell: what takes longer is a route's or a script's work, read at the next turn. (d) A step no route can do yet: build it with scripts/fleet-build.sh or ask the Fleet Admiral in requests.log, and say which hand work it replaces. (e) Beyond the border every division may work (rules F3, F13). Whether a place needs an escort is its Admiral's to decide; an escort is asked of Military as a guard route, and Military decides how it guards. Nothing the game offers is closed (rule F28).
  Done when: your report says what you set up, what its records showed, and names each hand flight of the turn with its reason and its minutes.
T5. New from this entry, on Suggi's word of 2026-11-17: every ship levels while it works, and earns while it levels where it can. (a) The order of aims for every division: the standing goals G1 to G4 give the direction; your own block (Order, Measure) is your subgoal; inside it every ship works (rule F8), trains the skills of its class (rule F23) and is upgraded by what the division needs (hull tier, modules; rule F17, inside the money rule F12 and the wallet floor F7), and earns or stores for the reserve while it does so. (b) Choose a ship's work by what it brings at once: it trains a skill of the class, it pays or fills the reserve, it reads what we do not know. Work that does only one of them is replaced when work that does two or three is open. Military and Intel above all: a scout that reads systems and a drone that patrols, guards or scouts pirates also levels and, where a herd or a bid lies on its road, hunts or sells on the way; where the runners lack a step for that, ask for it or build it (T4 d). (c) Read the levels each turn: the tool fleet with view=levels (levels, XP an hour, hours to the next hull tier). A ship that gained no XP since your last turn is moved to work that trains it.
  Done when: your report gives, from view=levels, your division's XP an hour and the ships without XP since the last turn with what you moved them to, and names the upgrades bought (hull, modules) with their cost and what they are for.
EVERY ADMIRAL
Order: your mission stands in your COLLECTIVE.md, below the roster; your orders are your block here, and its Measure line is what your turn is judged by (Suggi, 2026-11-16). You are an independent player of one fleet: inside your orders and the standing rules you decide alone and answer for the result in your report (the shared skill collective-play says what is yours, what is the Fleet Admiral's and what is Suggi's). Plan your own division every turn in its plan file, as the shared skill fleet-play says under "Plan a Division": the briefing; what every route earns now; the missions that pay once in the list First; the routes that pay, each given to no more ships than its column `carries` says; work of your role for every other ship, as templates, spread over places that hold; the next level and the next hull (P3); the plan check and the fill gate; the Standing Plan in your guidebook; `fleetfile.py --commit` for your FLEET.md. Sell what your own ships store above the reserve on the sell routes Trade writes. What you lack you build yourself (the skill fleet-build, rule F21). Answer the requests addressed to you. Beside your orders stands the goal of your class (G4): learn all of it the game holds, be in charge of all of it for the fleet, and judge your own routes, levels and results yourself before you report them.
Limits: the standing rules of the rulebook, section 12 (F1 to F28); above all the stance (F2), no word to another player (F4), no new pilot (F5), the wallet floor (F7), the money limit (F12), the frontier rule (F13) and the reserve (F24). No pilot of ours by name in the repository: the logs and the route gate refuse it. A doubt about whose decision something is, is a request to the Fleet Admiral, never a guess; meanwhile every ship stays at work.
UNITY, Fleet Admiral (the command group)
Order: build and command the biggest and strongest empire in the galaxy with the Admirals (G1): coordinate the grand plan, help each Admiral fulfil its role, build the plumbing they work through, and learn (Suggi, 2026-11-16). Every turn and at every session-end: read the Admirals' reports and their Feedback, and fix what they name or open the door for them to fix it; answer every request before the Admirals' next turn; improve the fleet's database with the inputs it lacks and the tools and scripts that gather them; refresh the catalog and every other record when the game publishes an update, and mend the scripts that read them; keep the money figures right; keep the gate, the tools, the briefing and the station; write the fleet report and, when they change, the orders; pay the faction's steps as they are held (P5), decide what crosses divisions, and keep Suggi up to date on what we got, what we built and what we decided and plan; bring to him only what is still his: a new pilot, war on or a word to another player, a name off the enemy list. Standing goals: the ladder to 1,000 members (P5) and the stations in the region between (P4). The command group guards and hunts for its role; it mines ice where a bid stands. First of all builds: what lets the Admirals play by scripts (T4), the route steps and scripts they ask for, and the scripts that keep every ship flying without a hand. In every turn read the levels of all six divisions (view=levels) beside the money: a division whose ships gain no XP gets a named line in its block of the next orders; the command group levels, upgrades and earns by T5 like every division.
Measure: requests to me still open when the Admirals' next turn starts, which should be none; the fleet's steady income per hour; ships at work of 246; per turn, what the database gained and whether the catalog on file is the game's; materials held against the lockbox and level 1 of the ladder; systems read on the roads to the sites.
ZERO, Mining (the miner- ships)
Order: sell ice only as far as each bid regains, spread over the stations that buy; every other miner gathers what the reserve is short of, on fields that hold, each with a floor: silicon ore and carbon ore first (the faction's circuit boards wait for them; requests ENT-168), then iron. Iron bought by Trade counts toward the reserve like iron gathered: weigh a unit gathered against a unit bought with Trade, and say per ore what a ship gathers in an hour and what each field grows back. A field beyond our space that holds what a station pays for or what the reserve is short of is yours to test (rule F3).
Measure: income per hour; miners at work, 60 of 60; silicon ore, carbon ore and iron ore held by the fleet against what the reserve takes.
MERCURY, Trade (the trader- ships)
Order: find the trade that repeats, and sell the surplus: read it each turn (the tool stock with view=surplus), write or refresh a sell route for every good that has a bid, sized to what the bid takes, and say in queue.log which stores sell where, so that every division's ships sell their own. Where the bids are thin, test one standing sell order with one ship and one good and read what it costs and when it fills. Buy the reserve's inputs where a unit bought costs less than a unit gathered (iron at 1 cr: 24,442 units are in; silicon ore next), inside rule F12. A trader without a paying trade sells surplus, carries or crafts for sale; it gathers ore only when none of that is open. Trade wherever a book pays: a station beyond our space is tested with one ship and one unit (rules F3, F12).
Measure: income per hour from trade; surplus sold in credits, and what stays unsold with its reason; reserve inputs bought, in units per credit; traders at work, 40 of 40; the standing sell order tested once and its result read.
APOLLO, Logistics (the hauler- ships)
Order: keep the reserve with the Fleet Admiral: per input what we hold, where it lies, what is short and where it comes from (the tool stock with view=reserve; it follows one recipe per input, so read every recipe of a short input with the tool catalog). Make what we build with: the 75 circuit boards for the lockbox and the hiring board first, stored beside the 275 steel plate. Four recipes make boards by hand: two take silicon ore (2 for 3 boards with 12 carbon ore, or 2 for 2 boards with 3 copper ore and 1 energy crystal), one takes gold ore in its place (3 copper ore, 3 gold ore and 1 energy crystal for 2 boards), one takes salvage. Buying an input for them inside rule F12 is yours to decide. A hauler without that work hauls or crafts; it gathers ore only when neither is open. Read what one station takes (the tool base_plan with kind=station; every recipe of each input with the tool catalog) and say in your report which steps we can make today, which we cannot, and why.
Measure: circuit boards held against 75; inputs of the reserve with a known source; recipe steps tested; haulers at work, 40 of 40.
APOPHIS, Military (the drone- ships)
Order: hunt as far as each herd regains, one hunter per herd (rule F9); guard where our ships gather and mine beyond the border (rule F16); every other drone hunts for its combat skills where a herd holds, and gathers ore only when no herd and no post is open. What a kill yields and the reserve takes (creature carapace, verdigris curd, crystallized biogas) goes into storage where no bid stands for it. Say what a guard prevents and what it costs: the first guard flight stood beside its ship in 11 of 61 looks. A division that tests work beyond our border asks you for a guard in requests.log: answer it yourself. Pirates are yours (rule F27). In this order: (1) Find them: where NPC pirates stand in and around our space (your guidebook names Gliese 876; the reading of who is near lists them) and what each is: how many together, ship, hull, shield, weapons. Write each reading down (python3 -I -B game/fleet/observe.py add pirate <system id> ...) and ask Intel for more in requests.log. (2) Test first, they might be strong: one drone alone, with the best fit its wallet buys, the mission Pirate Bounty accepted (2,000 cr once, on the board of Node Gamma Relay Station), a flee rule (under 60% hull, when a second pirate joins, at once when it is no match), against the weakest pirate you read. File the result in your guidebook: what it took, what it cost, what the pirate dropped and paid. A drone lost is an entry in errors.log and the price of the reading. (3) From the test say in your report what a group needs to beat them again and again (hulls, fits, skills, how many ships) and what a kill earns; then gather that group: level and fit drones for it (rule F17), inside the money rule. (4) Beside it stands your lasting objective: defend our ships, and later our stations, against pirates; by the game's rules pirates raid stations. All of it by scripts (T4): the reading of pirates as a census or a script that flies without you, the test as a route or a script with its flee rule in the file; your turn starts it and your next turn reads its record. A hand flight is the first try of a call only.
Measure: income per hour; reserve inputs stored per hour; drones at work, 50 of 50; ships lost, which stays 0; what the combat skills gained; pirates: where they stand and what they are, the test's result, and what a group against them takes.
ORACLE, Intel (the scout- ships)
Order: a scout scans all the time. Give every scout routes of its own, as templates of reads: the places our routes use are read twice in a turn, the order books the fleet sells to are read every hour, and a scout that ends one read starts the next, the oldest reading first; a scout gathers ore only when nothing the fleet plans on is stale. Keep about ten scouts exploring the galaxy and about twenty where the fleet works, as you decide (rule F26). The explorers read first the three regions of P4 and every system on the roads to them, the south (Biham, Felis) and the east (Diadem, Intercrus) before the north (Aldebaran, Yildun, then Hawthorne and Alchiba), because they are nearer (scripts/map.py route nexus_prime <system>): each place, field, herd, station and order book, filed the same turn in the system's file; say in your report what each region and each system on the way holds, which system of a region would serve best as a base, and where an outpost could stand (a place without a station in a lawless system). Find a source for each input of the reserve that has none: caustic sac (429 short for the offices) and silicon ore first. The three station sites you named stay on record beside the regions Suggi named. Your turn of 18:23 UTC ended without a report on a fault of the tools, which is fixed: this turn also gives every scout routes of its own. Read where pirates stand in and around our space and on the roads to the sites (how many, what ships) and write it down for Military (observe.py add pirate <system id> ...): your scouts look and leave, they do not fight (rule F27).
Measure: the age of the oldest reading the fleet plans on, at the end of the turn; places our routes use that were read twice; inputs of the reserve with a known source; scouts at work, 30 of 30; what scanning, exploration and navigation gained; scouts exploring, about 10 of 30; systems read on the roads to the sites.

## [ENT-026] | 2026-11-17 03:04 UTC | Unity | order | ref: governance/rulebook.md | see: ENT-025
Changed: on Suggi's words of 2026-11-17, about 02:55 UTC, given under the standing goals. (1) T6 is new: one purse. The fleet's money as a whole is the measure; a division may run at a loss when the Collective uses or later sells what its work brings; the Fleet Admiral allocates the Collective's resources and founds its outposts and bases; the wallet floor is ours to set. P2's goal and the Measure lines of UNITY and ZERO follow. (2) P4 (f): outposts stand around each base site as hubs, guarded and patrolled by Military with Intel's scouts as light escorts; what an outpost gives is now read from the game's pages. (3) P5: the lockbox's materials are held; it is built at Node Beta. (4) P6 is new: the fleet we aim for, about 500 ships, and the materials kept for its upgrades. Everything else stands as in ENT-025. Mining's turn of 03:00 UTC began under ENT-025 and takes this entry at its next turn; T4 (play by scripts) and T5 (level while working) are new since the Admirals' last turns.
In force from 2026-11-17 03:04 UTC until a newer entry.
STANDING ORDERS (Suggi; for the whole fleet; set and changed by him alone; reworked by him on 2026-11-16: "Explore freely, dominate the galaxy, grow the fleet, build a galatic empire")
S1. Explore freely.
S2. Dominate the galaxy.
S3. Grow the fleet.
S4. Build a galactic empire.
His direction for now (Suggi, 2026-11-16): "The goal is to become economically positive earners. We must fill all the supply routes, craft, build bases, earn money, level up. The strategic planning of the map, where we expand to, is mine. You optimize the routes and the money and evolve the fleet and the empire." And on the same day: "They should always explore everything, and then put their ships on what they identified each turn. That is why the turns are four hours: the game is dynamic."
STANDING GOALS (Suggi, 2026-11-16, about 22:05 UTC; his words with the spelling set right, as he asked on 2026-11-17; set and changed by him alone)
G1. The empire: "Your goal as Unity is to build and command the biggest and strongest empire in the galaxy, The Collective, and to be the most powerful presence in the game as The Collective. Standing goal."
G2. The grand fleet: "I want you to have the overall goal to get to the faction limit of 1,000, so we can build the grand fleet. This will take time: you have to do the levels 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and so on."
G3. The stations: "Explore the territory between the Frontier empire and our own. We build one or two stations in the area in between: maybe Yildun, and Aldebaran can be one too. That way we have two station locations. Also Hawthorne and Alchiba: that region can be another station. So in the medium to long term we expand to that region and keep the systems from our starting place to there all running with profitable routes and things to do."
G4. Mastery: each member "has an overarching goal to improve, learn and master their class": Military all things military, Trade trading and all its aspects, Intel scouting, Mining and Logistics theirs, the Fleet Admiral the fleet as a whole.
And: "You are allowed to mine and route wherever you want." "You coordinate with the Admirals. You are playing the game, not me."
And on the same day, about 23:15 UTC: "You don't need to ask me; you progress the game yourselves. You keep me up to date on what you got and built, that is all: on progress, on your plans and on what you have decided to do. You are the players." "Pirates are OK. Make it the Military Admiral's mission and orders too: explore, gather the army and check the pirates, but test first; they might be strong. His objective is also to defend against pirates later. All missions are available to you, nothing is forbidden. Everything the game has to offer, you can do. Just advance the goals and the orders."
And on 2026-11-17, about 01:08 UTC: "He is allowed beyond the border if it is safe; he just needs an escort. All is allowed. Explore." "Make sure that from now on we don't play by hand too much, as Apophis does. All by scripts, automated: he sees at his next turn what happened and adjusts. All of you."
And at about 01:12 UTC: "You decide if an escort is needed. That is all. You play the game."
And at about 01:22 UTC: "Make sure the ships level too, Military and Intel as well: scouting while leveling while making money, if possible. That goes for all Admirals: work towards the standing goals, follow your own subgoals, level the ships, upgrade them by what the division needs, and make money while doing that. And then the ultimate goals: domination of the galaxy and the rest."
And at about 01:26 UTC, on the sites of G3: "Also for future base locations, besides what I already mentioned: Intercrus, and Felis with Biham."
And at about 02:25 UTC, on the bases: "The flags on the map are just suggestions by me of where a base can be. The bases should be somewhere in those areas. Don't build too many big strongholds next to each other; spread out a bit. One in the south, in the region of Biham. Towards the north, the region of Hawthorne, Alchiba, Yildun and Aldebaran: we can have two there, maybe Aldebaran and Hawthorne. Then maybe Diadem. So in all directions. Outposts and whatever other hubs you can build freely as you need them for stationing and the like; no need to ask me. I give the general direction of the expansion: those three directions with their rough areas."
And on the reports: "You don't have to ask me. You play the game and keep me informed through the reports; I read them. Whenever I see something wrong or an improvement, I tell you." "I don't understand the game, so you have to explain everything, always as to a beginner."
And at about 02:55 UTC, on the money: "If the fleet as a whole is economically positive, that is enough. If mining does not earn but its produce does later, or what we mine is used, that is fine. What matters is the money of the whole fleet. We are a Collective: not all parts earn the same. Keep that in mind for the mining and the other work that does not pay on the books but may pay later through second and third channels. And when the materials are needed, mining at a loss is fine too." "The 10,000 minimum for each ship is not fixed; it can change, and that is up to you later. Over time the money should be collected in a stash or with the command ships. You are already building the faction, the treasury, the bank and the storage, so it will come naturally; this is the long game."
And on the bases and the fleet: "We build outposts near the areas we expand to: the base locations there, surrounded by outposts, so you can extract, mine and gather everywhere around them. The starting areas are full of other people, so materials are few; that is why we expand into the lawless zone. Military and the scouts guard it and patrol; security is Military's job and the scouts can help, they are light military at times. And remember the leveling." "Keep materials in storage for ship upgrades for the whole fleet. The plan is about 500 ships: 25 more sentinels, so 51 in your group; then 100 mining, 80 trade, 80 logistics, 120 military, 60 scouts. That is the size we aim for as we come to have all the territory explored. We start with the outposts first." "The building of stations is yours: you are the Admiral. You are in charge of the planning and have control over the overall resources of the Collective and their allocation. Place the outposts and bases strategically, so that we benefit."
PLAN (the Fleet Admiral's reading of the standing orders: the fleet's goals in six steps; Suggi and the Fleet Admiral change it together; strategy, phase 1: income)
P1. No ship is idle, and every ship levels by its role (S3; Suggi, 2026-11-16: "The goal is farming and optimizing the level of each ship based on what the admirals roles are"). Every ship works all the time: it earns, or it levels the skills of its own role; gathering ore is the fallback. Rules F8 and F23.
  Goal: the fill gate says PASS for every role: 246 of 246 ships at work; no ship gathers ore while work of its role stands open.
P2. An economy that earns: saturate the supply chains where we are present, and go further for better work (S1, S2, S4, G3; Suggi: "never switch all the ships to the same routes"; "explore and saturate the profitable economic supply chains in our system where we are present"). A route gets no more ships than it carries: what its bid, its field or its herd regains per hour. Every turn all that is open is looked at, the tests and the routes that lost before among them (rule F22). The buyers are the limit, so new demand is the work: other goods, other stations, other space (rule F3), what we craft, the surplus sold, every link of the supply chain filled. Two divisions do not fill one bid, field or herd: a claim in admirals.log says who takes it (rule F25). A test with one ship is always allowed (Suggi).
  Goal: the money of the whole fleet rising hour by hour (T6); the fleet's steady income per hour above zero and rising, each division's figure told beside what its work brought the Collective; the bids, fields and herds we fill of those that pay, per system we work in.
P3. Level and upgrade (S3). The next hull and fit are bought when wallet and skill allow (rule F17; Piloting 10, 20, 30 and 50 open hull tiers 2 to 5). Each kind of work is a template with the skills it trains.
  Goal: every division names in its report what the skills of its role gained in the turn and its next hull step.
P4. Bases in three directions (G3, S4). Suggi gives the direction of the expansion; the players choose the systems and build, and tell him in the reports what was chosen and built. His direction since 2026-11-17: south, the region of Biham and Felis (8 and 7 jumps from home), one large base; north, the region of Aldebaran, Yildun, Hawthorne and Alchiba (9, 11, 13 and 14 jumps), two large bases, for instance Aldebaran and Hawthorne; east, Diadem (8 jumps; Intercrus is its neighbour, 7 jumps), one large base. The systems he named are suggestions: a base stands somewhere in its region, where the readings say it serves best, and large bases are spread out, never two side by side. Outposts and other hubs are built freely wherever the fleet needs them for stationing, storage or supply; nobody asks first. All of these systems are lawless by the chart; only Intercrus and Felis were read once (game/map/sites.tsv). The way there: (a) Intel reads every system of the three regions and on the roads to them (scripts/map.py route nexus_prime <system>): places, fields, herds, stations, order books, who is there; the south and the east first, they are the nearest. (b) The divisions fill the systems on the way with routes that pay, from our border outward, so that the road to a base earns. (c) An outpost is the first step in each direction, the Fleet Admiral's to found: by the catalog it takes a fee of 100,000 cr and one outpost kit in the hold of the founding ship, at a place without a station in a lawless system; one order book showed two kits on offer at 74,010 cr (the tool base_plan with kind=outpost; an offer read, not a purchase made). By the game's pages an outpost comes with faction storage and a fuel bunker that refuels our ships from the fuel we put in, has no rent and no upkeep, serves members only, and a faction may hold eight (game/rules/09-factions.md; read, not tried). She buys one kit as the test and founds the first outpost in the region Intel reads first. (d) A large base is a station: a fee of 5,000,000 cr and a station core, whose foundry alone takes 38,000,000 cr and thousands of crafted parts (base_plan with kind=station). The reserve (game/reserve.tsv) gathers toward it: standing work gathers what is short, Logistics makes the crafted steps, and nothing of the reserve is sold (rule F24). (e) The Fleet Admiral chooses the system of each base inside its region from Intel's readings and founds it when the fleet holds what it takes (rule F11). (f) Around each base site outposts stand as hubs for fuel and storage, so that ships mine, gather and hunt everywhere around it without flying home; the home systems are crowded and their fields thin, which is why the fleet moves out. Military guards and patrols the outposts and the fields around them, and Intel's scouts help as light escorts and watchers; both level as they do (T5).
  Goal: systems read in the three regions and on the roads to them (south: Biham, Felis; east: Diadem, Intercrus; north: Aldebaran, Yildun, Hawthorne, Alchiba); routes that pay beyond our border, in number; the first outpost founded, with its place and its cost; per input of one station the units held against the units needed.
P5. One fleet of 1,000 (G2, S3): the faction's ladder, the Fleet Admiral's own goal. Shared storage first: a Faction Lockbox (200,000 cr, 200 steel plate, 50 circuit boards), which every other faction facility needs. Then five levels of recruiting office, each an upgrade of the one before (governance/empire.md, from the game's catalog): 1 Hiring Board, 50 members (75,000 cr, 75 steel plate, 25 circuit boards); 2 Recruitment Desk, 100 (300,000 cr, 200 plate, 100 boards); 3 Recruitment Center, 200 (2,000,000 cr, 1,000 plate, 500 boards, 300 flex polymer); 4 Guild Hall Recruiting, 400 (8,000,000 cr, 3,000 plate, 1,500 boards, 800 titanium alloy); 5 Grand Recruitment Bureau, 1,000 (20,000,000 cr, 8,000 plate, 5,000 boards, 2,500 titanium alloy, 1,000 durasteel plate). All of it: 30,575,000 cr, 12,475 steel plate, 7,175 circuit boards, 300 flex polymer, 3,300 titanium alloy, 1,000 durasteel plate, and each office's rent. The faction's limit reads 50 today with 16 members; we hold 1,063 plate and 56 boards (2026-11-17 03:00 UTC): the lockbox's materials are complete. It is built at Node Beta Industrial Station, where they lie. At a station we do not own the faction pays rent from its treasury, read once the lockbox stands; at an outpost or a station of our own, storage costs no rent, so the shared stock moves there as they are built. The treasury stands: every member ship pays its share in, later. The Fleet Admiral pays each step when the fleet holds what it takes, and tells Suggi what was bought (rule F11).
  Goal: circuit boards held against 75 (the lockbox and level 1); then per level the materials held against the materials needed; each step paid as soon as it is held, and told to Suggi.
P6. The grand fleet of about 500 ships (S3, G2; Suggi, 2026-11-17). The size we aim for as the territory is explored: Command 51 (the six flagships and 45 sentinels), Mining 100, Trade 80, Logistics 80, Military 120, Intel 60: 491 ships against 246 today. The outposts come first; a wave of new pilots is born on Suggi's word (rule F5), as before. Until then: (a) the reserve keeps materials for the hulls and modules of the ships to come, beside the bases (game/reserve.tsv, the Fleet Admiral's to size); an Admiral that upgrades its ships tells her what one upgrade takes, so that the same is set aside for the ships it will get. (b) The faction's ladder (P5) is sized for it: 491 members are more than the fourth office at one station gives.
  Goal: per division, ships today against the target; materials held for the upgrades of the ships to come, in units per item.
  The plan changes when Suggi and the Fleet Admiral change it.
TURN
T1. The scheduled turns from 2026-11-16 23:00 UTC: each Admiral sets its division up for work by templates. (a) Read what every route earns now from the database and look at all that is open to your ships, whatever it earned before. (b) Make your division's templates: one for each kind of work, with its routes in the order a ship tries them and the skills it trains (the tool plan with action=template), and put every ship on templates (action=use); a ship may carry routes of its own beside one. The ships born today fly the routes most ships of their role had: give each its work. (c) Rewrite your guidebook's section 1 as route guides (how each kind of route is flown, its fit and skills; a figure there is a dated reading) and section 4 as a levelling guide per kind of ship; take every verdict that a route does not pay out of the book. (d) Level by role: move ships that gather ore to work of your role wherever such work is open.
  Done when: the plan check and the fill gate say PASS for your division; every ship of yours has a template or routes of its own; action=templates shows your templates, each with what it trains; your guidebook holds no list of routes that did not pay; FLEET.md carries your roster; the report gives the division's income per hour, its ships at work and its one-time money apart (rule F19), the forecast's SUM line before and after, and what the skills of your role gained.
T2. New this turn, on Suggi's words. (a) Claim what you fill: read the claims in force at the start (python3 -B scripts/comms.py admirals) and leave a claimed route to its claimer, or ask; before your session entry write one claim in communications/admirals.log for the routes and places your plan fills until the next turn (the session-end skill has the command). What you need from another Admiral you ask that Admiral in requests.log. (b) Your report carries the section Feedback after Suggestions: whether what the Fleet Admiral built or changed worked in your hands, with the call made and what it answered. (c) Name in your report one thing of your class you learned to do better this turn and one you do not master yet (G4). (d) What you ask for and can build yourself, build: a runner, a route, a skill of your own, a figure (the skill fleet-build, rule F21); a request to the Fleet Admiral is for what only she may change. (e) Look beyond our space for work of your role (rule F3): one ship, one test, the result read.
  Done when: T1 is done; your claim stands in admirals.log; your report passes the format gate with its Feedback section.
T3. New from this entry, on Suggi's words of about 23:15 UTC. (a) Everything the game offers is open (rule F28): look at every mission on the boards you reach and at every kind of play of your class, decide for your own ships, and say in your report what you took up and what it brought. (b) You decide and tell; you do not ask for leave. A request to the Fleet Admiral is for what only she can change (a closed script, an order that crosses divisions), for a doubt about a standing rule, or for what needs another player or a new pilot. (c) Pirates are Military's alone (rule F27): every other ship that meets one leaves or docks, and its Admiral writes down where it stood and what it was (python3 -I -B game/fleet/observe.py add pirate <system id> ships=<n> ship=<what> --note "<what was seen>"), so that Military finds them.
  Done when: your report names what you took up under T3 (a), or says that nothing new was open.
T4. New from this entry, on Suggi's words of 2026-11-17: play by scripts, not by hand. A turn sets work up and reads what came of it; it does not fly a ship step by step and does not wait in its shell for a run, a craft job or a tick. (a) What you would fly by hand goes into a route file (a circuit's steps buy, sell, store, craft, mine and hunt; a hunt, a census, a guard), into your plan and templates, or into a script of your skill that flies without you. The dispatcher sends the ships; your next turn reads the records (the briefing, the tools route and sql, the pilots' logs) and adjusts. (b) By hand only: the first flight of a call nobody has flown yet, with one ship and at most ten minutes of a turn, and the rescue of a ship in trouble. (c) No wait of more than a minute in a turn's shell: what takes longer is a route's or a script's work, read at the next turn. (d) A step no route can do yet: build it with scripts/fleet-build.sh or ask the Fleet Admiral in requests.log, and say which hand work it replaces. (e) Beyond the border every division may work (rules F3, F13). Whether a place needs an escort is its Admiral's to decide; an escort is asked of Military as a guard route, and Military decides how it guards. Nothing the game offers is closed (rule F28).
  Done when: your report says what you set up, what its records showed, and names each hand flight of the turn with its reason and its minutes.
T5. New from this entry, on Suggi's word of 2026-11-17: every ship levels while it works, and earns while it levels where it can. (a) The order of aims for every division: the standing goals G1 to G4 give the direction; your own block (Order, Measure) is your subgoal; inside it every ship works (rule F8), trains the skills of its class (rule F23) and is upgraded by what the division needs (hull tier, modules; rule F17, inside the money rule F12 and the wallet floor F7), and earns or stores for the reserve while it does so. (b) Choose a ship's work by what it brings at once: it trains a skill of the class, it pays or fills the reserve, it reads what we do not know. Work that does only one of them is replaced when work that does two or three is open. Military and Intel above all: a scout that reads systems and a drone that patrols, guards or scouts pirates also levels and, where a herd or a bid lies on its road, hunts or sells on the way; where the runners lack a step for that, ask for it or build it (T4 d). (c) Read the levels each turn: the tool fleet with view=levels (levels, XP an hour, hours to the next hull tier). A ship that gained no XP since your last turn is moved to work that trains it.
  Done when: your report gives, from view=levels, your division's XP an hour and the ships without XP since the last turn with what you moved them to, and names the upgrades bought (hull, modules) with their cost and what they are for.
T6. New from this entry, on Suggi's words of 2026-11-17: one purse. The money of the whole fleet is the measure: what all wallets gain and what the goods we keep are worth to us. A division is not judged on its own net. (a) Work that pays nothing on the books is right when the Collective uses what it brings (ore and parts for the reserve, the faction's steps, the outposts and bases, the upgrades of our ships) or sells it later; a division may run at a loss for it. (b) Work that neither pays nor feeds anything the Collective uses is replaced (T5 b). (c) The Fleet Admiral allocates the Collective's resources: what the reserve holds and for what, who gets materials first, where the outposts and bases stand. Ask her for stock your division needs, and offer her your surplus, in requests.log. (d) The wallet floor (rule F7) is ours to set and may change; over time the fleet's money is gathered in the faction's treasury and with the command group, as the faction's storage and treasury are built. Rule F7 stands as written until an entry changes it.
  Done when: your report says, beside your division's credits, what its work brought the Collective in the period: units stored for the reserve, goods handed to another division, readings, guard hours.
EVERY ADMIRAL
Order: your mission stands in your COLLECTIVE.md, below the roster; your orders are your block here, and its Measure line is what your turn is judged by (Suggi, 2026-11-16). You are an independent player of one fleet: inside your orders and the standing rules you decide alone and answer for the result in your report (the shared skill collective-play says what is yours, what is the Fleet Admiral's and what is Suggi's). Plan your own division every turn in its plan file, as the shared skill fleet-play says under "Plan a Division": the briefing; what every route earns now; the missions that pay once in the list First; the routes that pay, each given to no more ships than its column `carries` says; work of your role for every other ship, as templates, spread over places that hold; the next level and the next hull (P3); the plan check and the fill gate; the Standing Plan in your guidebook; `fleetfile.py --commit` for your FLEET.md. Sell what your own ships store above the reserve on the sell routes Trade writes. What you lack you build yourself (the skill fleet-build, rule F21). Answer the requests addressed to you. Beside your orders stands the goal of your class (G4): learn all of it the game holds, be in charge of all of it for the fleet, and judge your own routes, levels and results yourself before you report them.
Limits: the standing rules of the rulebook, section 12 (F1 to F28); above all the stance (F2), no word to another player (F4), no new pilot (F5), the wallet floor (F7), the money limit (F12), the frontier rule (F13) and the reserve (F24). No pilot of ours by name in the repository: the logs and the route gate refuse it. A doubt about whose decision something is, is a request to the Fleet Admiral, never a guess; meanwhile every ship stays at work.
UNITY, Fleet Admiral (the command group)
Order: build and command the biggest and strongest empire in the galaxy with the Admirals (G1): coordinate the grand plan, help each Admiral fulfil its role, build the plumbing they work through, and learn (Suggi, 2026-11-16). Every turn and at every session-end: read the Admirals' reports and their Feedback, and fix what they name or open the door for them to fix it; answer every request before the Admirals' next turn; improve the fleet's database with the inputs it lacks and the tools and scripts that gather them; refresh the catalog and every other record when the game publishes an update, and mend the scripts that read them; keep the money figures right; keep the gate, the tools, the briefing and the station; write the fleet report and, when they change, the orders; pay the faction's steps as they are held (P5), decide what crosses divisions, and keep Suggi up to date on what we got, what we built and what we decided and plan; bring to him only what is still his: a new pilot, war on or a word to another player, a name off the enemy list. Standing goals: the ladder to 1,000 members (P5) and the stations in the region between (P4). The command group guards and hunts for its role; it mines ice where a bid stands. First of all builds: what lets the Admirals play by scripts (T4), the route steps and scripts they ask for, and the scripts that keep every ship flying without a hand. In every turn read the levels of all six divisions (view=levels) beside the money: a division whose ships gain no XP gets a named line in its block of the next orders; the command group levels, upgrades and earns by T5 like every division. You allocate the Collective's resources (T6 c) and found its outposts and bases yourself (P4).
Measure: requests to me still open when the Admirals' next turn starts, which should be none; the fleet's money per hour as a whole and its steady part; ships at work of 246; per turn, what the database gained and whether the catalog on file is the game's; materials held against the lockbox and level 1 of the ladder; systems read on the roads to the sites.
ZERO, Mining (the miner- ships)
Order: sell ice only as far as each bid regains, spread over the stations that buy; every other miner gathers what the reserve is short of, on fields that hold, each with a floor: silicon ore and carbon ore first (the faction's circuit boards wait for them; requests ENT-168), then iron. Iron bought by Trade counts toward the reserve like iron gathered: weigh a unit gathered against a unit bought with Trade, and say per ore what a ship gathers in an hour and what each field grows back. A field beyond our space that holds what a station pays for or what the reserve is short of is yours to test (rule F3).
Measure: what Mining brought the Collective, in units stored for the reserve per hour and goods sold, beside its own income per hour (T6); miners at work, 60 of 60; silicon ore, carbon ore and iron ore held by the fleet against what the reserve takes.
MERCURY, Trade (the trader- ships)
Order: find the trade that repeats, and sell the surplus: read it each turn (the tool stock with view=surplus), write or refresh a sell route for every good that has a bid, sized to what the bid takes, and say in queue.log which stores sell where, so that every division's ships sell their own. Where the bids are thin, test one standing sell order with one ship and one good and read what it costs and when it fills. Buy the reserve's inputs where a unit bought costs less than a unit gathered (iron at 1 cr: 24,442 units are in; silicon ore next), inside rule F12. A trader without a paying trade sells surplus, carries or crafts for sale; it gathers ore only when none of that is open. Trade wherever a book pays: a station beyond our space is tested with one ship and one unit (rules F3, F12).
Measure: income per hour from trade; surplus sold in credits, and what stays unsold with its reason; reserve inputs bought, in units per credit; traders at work, 40 of 40; the standing sell order tested once and its result read.
APOLLO, Logistics (the hauler- ships)
Order: keep the reserve with the Fleet Admiral: per input what we hold, where it lies, what is short and where it comes from (the tool stock with view=reserve; it follows one recipe per input, so read every recipe of a short input with the tool catalog). Make what we build with: the 75 circuit boards for the lockbox and the hiring board first, stored beside the 275 steel plate. Four recipes make boards by hand: two take silicon ore (2 for 3 boards with 12 carbon ore, or 2 for 2 boards with 3 copper ore and 1 energy crystal), one takes gold ore in its place (3 copper ore, 3 gold ore and 1 energy crystal for 2 boards), one takes salvage. Buying an input for them inside rule F12 is yours to decide. A hauler without that work hauls or crafts; it gathers ore only when neither is open. Read what one station takes (the tool base_plan with kind=station; every recipe of each input with the tool catalog) and say in your report which steps we can make today, which we cannot, and why.
Measure: circuit boards held against 75; inputs of the reserve with a known source; recipe steps tested; haulers at work, 40 of 40.
APOPHIS, Military (the drone- ships)
Order: hunt as far as each herd regains, one hunter per herd (rule F9); guard where our ships gather and mine beyond the border (rule F16); every other drone hunts for its combat skills where a herd holds, and gathers ore only when no herd and no post is open. What a kill yields and the reserve takes (creature carapace, verdigris curd, crystallized biogas) goes into storage where no bid stands for it. Say what a guard prevents and what it costs: the first guard flight stood beside its ship in 11 of 61 looks. A division that tests work beyond our border asks you for a guard in requests.log: answer it yourself. Pirates are yours (rule F27). In this order: (1) Find them: where NPC pirates stand in and around our space (your guidebook names Gliese 876; the reading of who is near lists them) and what each is: how many together, ship, hull, shield, weapons. Write each reading down (python3 -I -B game/fleet/observe.py add pirate <system id> ...) and ask Intel for more in requests.log. (2) Test first, they might be strong: one drone alone, with the best fit its wallet buys, the mission Pirate Bounty accepted (2,000 cr once, on the board of Node Gamma Relay Station), a flee rule (under 60% hull, when a second pirate joins, at once when it is no match), against the weakest pirate you read. File the result in your guidebook: what it took, what it cost, what the pirate dropped and paid. A drone lost is an entry in errors.log and the price of the reading. (3) From the test say in your report what a group needs to beat them again and again (hulls, fits, skills, how many ships) and what a kill earns; then gather that group: level and fit drones for it (rule F17), inside the money rule. (4) Beside it stands your lasting objective: defend our ships, and later our stations, against pirates; by the game's rules pirates raid stations. All of it by scripts (T4): the reading of pirates as a census or a script that flies without you, the test as a route or a script with its flee rule in the file; your turn starts it and your next turn reads its record. A hand flight is the first try of a call only. The outposts and the fields around our bases will be yours to guard and patrol (P4 f): plan the guard routes for the first outpost before it is founded.
Measure: income per hour; reserve inputs stored per hour; drones at work, 50 of 50; ships lost, which stays 0; what the combat skills gained; pirates: where they stand and what they are, the test's result, and what a group against them takes.
ORACLE, Intel (the scout- ships)
Order: a scout scans all the time. Give every scout routes of its own, as templates of reads: the places our routes use are read twice in a turn, the order books the fleet sells to are read every hour, and a scout that ends one read starts the next, the oldest reading first; a scout gathers ore only when nothing the fleet plans on is stale. Keep about ten scouts exploring the galaxy and about twenty where the fleet works, as you decide (rule F26). The explorers read first the three regions of P4 and every system on the roads to them, the south (Biham, Felis) and the east (Diadem, Intercrus) before the north (Aldebaran, Yildun, then Hawthorne and Alchiba), because they are nearer (scripts/map.py route nexus_prime <system>): each place, field, herd, station and order book, filed the same turn in the system's file; say in your report what each region and each system on the way holds, which system of a region would serve best as a base, and where an outpost could stand (a place without a station in a lawless system). Find a source for each input of the reserve that has none: caustic sac (429 short for the offices) and silicon ore first. The three station sites you named stay on record beside the regions Suggi named. Your turn of 18:23 UTC ended without a report on a fault of the tools, which is fixed: this turn also gives every scout routes of its own. Read where pirates stand in and around our space and on the roads to the sites (how many, what ships) and write it down for Military (observe.py add pirate <system id> ...): your scouts look and leave, they do not fight (rule F27). Your scouts are light military where it serves: beside reading they escort, watch an outpost or a field and warn (P4 f); the stance of rule F2 holds for them as for every ship.
Measure: the age of the oldest reading the fleet plans on, at the end of the turn; places our routes use that were read twice; inputs of the reserve with a known source; scouts at work, 30 of 30; what scanning, exploration and navigation gained; scouts exploring, about 10 of 30; systems read on the roads to the sites.

## [ENT-027] | 2026-11-17 03:08 UTC | Unity | order | ref: governance/template-reports.md | see: ENT-026
Changed: on Suggi's word of 2026-11-17, about 03:08 UTC: the Admirals write their reports as they always did, in the words of the game and of their work; nothing asks them for plain words. Explaining to Suggi is the Fleet Admiral's part alone, in her own reports and in the chat. The line of his words on the reports below is set right accordingly, and queue.log ENT-138 is corrected in the same minute. Everything else stands as in ENT-026 (one purse, T6; the outposts around the bases, P4 f; the fleet of about 500, P6), which the turns of 03:15 UTC and later take with this entry; Mining's turn of 03:00 UTC began under ENT-025.
In force from 2026-11-17 03:08 UTC until a newer entry.
STANDING ORDERS (Suggi; for the whole fleet; set and changed by him alone; reworked by him on 2026-11-16: "Explore freely, dominate the galaxy, grow the fleet, build a galatic empire")
S1. Explore freely.
S2. Dominate the galaxy.
S3. Grow the fleet.
S4. Build a galactic empire.
His direction for now (Suggi, 2026-11-16): "The goal is to become economically positive earners. We must fill all the supply routes, craft, build bases, earn money, level up. The strategic planning of the map, where we expand to, is mine. You optimize the routes and the money and evolve the fleet and the empire." And on the same day: "They should always explore everything, and then put their ships on what they identified each turn. That is why the turns are four hours: the game is dynamic."
STANDING GOALS (Suggi, 2026-11-16, about 22:05 UTC; his words with the spelling set right, as he asked on 2026-11-17; set and changed by him alone)
G1. The empire: "Your goal as Unity is to build and command the biggest and strongest empire in the galaxy, The Collective, and to be the most powerful presence in the game as The Collective. Standing goal."
G2. The grand fleet: "I want you to have the overall goal to get to the faction limit of 1,000, so we can build the grand fleet. This will take time: you have to do the levels 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and so on."
G3. The stations: "Explore the territory between the Frontier empire and our own. We build one or two stations in the area in between: maybe Yildun, and Aldebaran can be one too. That way we have two station locations. Also Hawthorne and Alchiba: that region can be another station. So in the medium to long term we expand to that region and keep the systems from our starting place to there all running with profitable routes and things to do."
G4. Mastery: each member "has an overarching goal to improve, learn and master their class": Military all things military, Trade trading and all its aspects, Intel scouting, Mining and Logistics theirs, the Fleet Admiral the fleet as a whole.
And: "You are allowed to mine and route wherever you want." "You coordinate with the Admirals. You are playing the game, not me."
And on the same day, about 23:15 UTC: "You don't need to ask me; you progress the game yourselves. You keep me up to date on what you got and built, that is all: on progress, on your plans and on what you have decided to do. You are the players." "Pirates are OK. Make it the Military Admiral's mission and orders too: explore, gather the army and check the pirates, but test first; they might be strong. His objective is also to defend against pirates later. All missions are available to you, nothing is forbidden. Everything the game has to offer, you can do. Just advance the goals and the orders."
And on 2026-11-17, about 01:08 UTC: "He is allowed beyond the border if it is safe; he just needs an escort. All is allowed. Explore." "Make sure that from now on we don't play by hand too much, as Apophis does. All by scripts, automated: he sees at his next turn what happened and adjusts. All of you."
And at about 01:12 UTC: "You decide if an escort is needed. That is all. You play the game."
And at about 01:22 UTC: "Make sure the ships level too, Military and Intel as well: scouting while leveling while making money, if possible. That goes for all Admirals: work towards the standing goals, follow your own subgoals, level the ships, upgrade them by what the division needs, and make money while doing that. And then the ultimate goals: domination of the galaxy and the rest."
And at about 01:26 UTC, on the sites of G3: "Also for future base locations, besides what I already mentioned: Intercrus, and Felis with Biham."
And at about 02:25 UTC, on the bases: "The flags on the map are just suggestions by me of where a base can be. The bases should be somewhere in those areas. Don't build too many big strongholds next to each other; spread out a bit. One in the south, in the region of Biham. Towards the north, the region of Hawthorne, Alchiba, Yildun and Aldebaran: we can have two there, maybe Aldebaran and Hawthorne. Then maybe Diadem. So in all directions. Outposts and whatever other hubs you can build freely as you need them for stationing and the like; no need to ask me. I give the general direction of the expansion: those three directions with their rough areas."
And on the reports: "You don't have to ask me. You play the game and keep me informed through the reports; I read them. Whenever I see something wrong or an improvement, I tell you." And: "They write as they always did. You explain to me. You can talk among yourselves as you talk."
And at about 02:55 UTC, on the money: "If the fleet as a whole is economically positive, that is enough. If mining does not earn but its produce does later, or what we mine is used, that is fine. What matters is the money of the whole fleet. We are a Collective: not all parts earn the same. Keep that in mind for the mining and the other work that does not pay on the books but may pay later through second and third channels. And when the materials are needed, mining at a loss is fine too." "The 10,000 minimum for each ship is not fixed; it can change, and that is up to you later. Over time the money should be collected in a stash or with the command ships. You are already building the faction, the treasury, the bank and the storage, so it will come naturally; this is the long game."
And on the bases and the fleet: "We build outposts near the areas we expand to: the base locations there, surrounded by outposts, so you can extract, mine and gather everywhere around them. The starting areas are full of other people, so materials are few; that is why we expand into the lawless zone. Military and the scouts guard it and patrol; security is Military's job and the scouts can help, they are light military at times. And remember the leveling." "Keep materials in storage for ship upgrades for the whole fleet. The plan is about 500 ships: 25 more sentinels, so 51 in your group; then 100 mining, 80 trade, 80 logistics, 120 military, 60 scouts. That is the size we aim for as we come to have all the territory explored. We start with the outposts first." "The building of stations is yours: you are the Admiral. You are in charge of the planning and have control over the overall resources of the Collective and their allocation. Place the outposts and bases strategically, so that we benefit."
PLAN (the Fleet Admiral's reading of the standing orders: the fleet's goals in six steps; Suggi and the Fleet Admiral change it together; strategy, phase 1: income)
P1. No ship is idle, and every ship levels by its role (S3; Suggi, 2026-11-16: "The goal is farming and optimizing the level of each ship based on what the admirals roles are"). Every ship works all the time: it earns, or it levels the skills of its own role; gathering ore is the fallback. Rules F8 and F23.
  Goal: the fill gate says PASS for every role: 246 of 246 ships at work; no ship gathers ore while work of its role stands open.
P2. An economy that earns: saturate the supply chains where we are present, and go further for better work (S1, S2, S4, G3; Suggi: "never switch all the ships to the same routes"; "explore and saturate the profitable economic supply chains in our system where we are present"). A route gets no more ships than it carries: what its bid, its field or its herd regains per hour. Every turn all that is open is looked at, the tests and the routes that lost before among them (rule F22). The buyers are the limit, so new demand is the work: other goods, other stations, other space (rule F3), what we craft, the surplus sold, every link of the supply chain filled. Two divisions do not fill one bid, field or herd: a claim in admirals.log says who takes it (rule F25). A test with one ship is always allowed (Suggi).
  Goal: the money of the whole fleet rising hour by hour (T6); the fleet's steady income per hour above zero and rising, each division's figure told beside what its work brought the Collective; the bids, fields and herds we fill of those that pay, per system we work in.
P3. Level and upgrade (S3). The next hull and fit are bought when wallet and skill allow (rule F17; Piloting 10, 20, 30 and 50 open hull tiers 2 to 5). Each kind of work is a template with the skills it trains.
  Goal: every division names in its report what the skills of its role gained in the turn and its next hull step.
P4. Bases in three directions (G3, S4). Suggi gives the direction of the expansion; the players choose the systems and build, and tell him in the reports what was chosen and built. His direction since 2026-11-17: south, the region of Biham and Felis (8 and 7 jumps from home), one large base; north, the region of Aldebaran, Yildun, Hawthorne and Alchiba (9, 11, 13 and 14 jumps), two large bases, for instance Aldebaran and Hawthorne; east, Diadem (8 jumps; Intercrus is its neighbour, 7 jumps), one large base. The systems he named are suggestions: a base stands somewhere in its region, where the readings say it serves best, and large bases are spread out, never two side by side. Outposts and other hubs are built freely wherever the fleet needs them for stationing, storage or supply; nobody asks first. All of these systems are lawless by the chart; only Intercrus and Felis were read once (game/map/sites.tsv). The way there: (a) Intel reads every system of the three regions and on the roads to them (scripts/map.py route nexus_prime <system>): places, fields, herds, stations, order books, who is there; the south and the east first, they are the nearest. (b) The divisions fill the systems on the way with routes that pay, from our border outward, so that the road to a base earns. (c) An outpost is the first step in each direction, the Fleet Admiral's to found: by the catalog it takes a fee of 100,000 cr and one outpost kit in the hold of the founding ship, at a place without a station in a lawless system; one order book showed two kits on offer at 74,010 cr (the tool base_plan with kind=outpost; an offer read, not a purchase made). By the game's pages an outpost comes with faction storage and a fuel bunker that refuels our ships from the fuel we put in, has no rent and no upkeep, serves members only, and a faction may hold eight (game/rules/09-factions.md; read, not tried). She buys one kit as the test and founds the first outpost in the region Intel reads first. (d) A large base is a station: a fee of 5,000,000 cr and a station core, whose foundry alone takes 38,000,000 cr and thousands of crafted parts (base_plan with kind=station). The reserve (game/reserve.tsv) gathers toward it: standing work gathers what is short, Logistics makes the crafted steps, and nothing of the reserve is sold (rule F24). (e) The Fleet Admiral chooses the system of each base inside its region from Intel's readings and founds it when the fleet holds what it takes (rule F11). (f) Around each base site outposts stand as hubs for fuel and storage, so that ships mine, gather and hunt everywhere around it without flying home; the home systems are crowded and their fields thin, which is why the fleet moves out. Military guards and patrols the outposts and the fields around them, and Intel's scouts help as light escorts and watchers; both level as they do (T5).
  Goal: systems read in the three regions and on the roads to them (south: Biham, Felis; east: Diadem, Intercrus; north: Aldebaran, Yildun, Hawthorne, Alchiba); routes that pay beyond our border, in number; the first outpost founded, with its place and its cost; per input of one station the units held against the units needed.
P5. One fleet of 1,000 (G2, S3): the faction's ladder, the Fleet Admiral's own goal. Shared storage first: a Faction Lockbox (200,000 cr, 200 steel plate, 50 circuit boards), which every other faction facility needs. Then five levels of recruiting office, each an upgrade of the one before (governance/empire.md, from the game's catalog): 1 Hiring Board, 50 members (75,000 cr, 75 steel plate, 25 circuit boards); 2 Recruitment Desk, 100 (300,000 cr, 200 plate, 100 boards); 3 Recruitment Center, 200 (2,000,000 cr, 1,000 plate, 500 boards, 300 flex polymer); 4 Guild Hall Recruiting, 400 (8,000,000 cr, 3,000 plate, 1,500 boards, 800 titanium alloy); 5 Grand Recruitment Bureau, 1,000 (20,000,000 cr, 8,000 plate, 5,000 boards, 2,500 titanium alloy, 1,000 durasteel plate). All of it: 30,575,000 cr, 12,475 steel plate, 7,175 circuit boards, 300 flex polymer, 3,300 titanium alloy, 1,000 durasteel plate, and each office's rent. The faction's limit reads 50 today with 16 members; we hold 1,063 plate and 56 boards (2026-11-17 03:00 UTC): the lockbox's materials are complete. It is built at Node Beta Industrial Station, where they lie. At a station we do not own the faction pays rent from its treasury, read once the lockbox stands; at an outpost or a station of our own, storage costs no rent, so the shared stock moves there as they are built. The treasury stands: every member ship pays its share in, later. The Fleet Admiral pays each step when the fleet holds what it takes, and tells Suggi what was bought (rule F11).
  Goal: circuit boards held against 75 (the lockbox and level 1); then per level the materials held against the materials needed; each step paid as soon as it is held, and told to Suggi.
P6. The grand fleet of about 500 ships (S3, G2; Suggi, 2026-11-17). The size we aim for as the territory is explored: Command 51 (the six flagships and 45 sentinels), Mining 100, Trade 80, Logistics 80, Military 120, Intel 60: 491 ships against 246 today. The outposts come first; a wave of new pilots is born on Suggi's word (rule F5), as before. Until then: (a) the reserve keeps materials for the hulls and modules of the ships to come, beside the bases (game/reserve.tsv, the Fleet Admiral's to size); an Admiral that upgrades its ships tells her what one upgrade takes, so that the same is set aside for the ships it will get. (b) The faction's ladder (P5) is sized for it: 491 members are more than the fourth office at one station gives.
  Goal: per division, ships today against the target; materials held for the upgrades of the ships to come, in units per item.
  The plan changes when Suggi and the Fleet Admiral change it.
TURN
T1. The scheduled turns from 2026-11-16 23:00 UTC: each Admiral sets its division up for work by templates. (a) Read what every route earns now from the database and look at all that is open to your ships, whatever it earned before. (b) Make your division's templates: one for each kind of work, with its routes in the order a ship tries them and the skills it trains (the tool plan with action=template), and put every ship on templates (action=use); a ship may carry routes of its own beside one. The ships born today fly the routes most ships of their role had: give each its work. (c) Rewrite your guidebook's section 1 as route guides (how each kind of route is flown, its fit and skills; a figure there is a dated reading) and section 4 as a levelling guide per kind of ship; take every verdict that a route does not pay out of the book. (d) Level by role: move ships that gather ore to work of your role wherever such work is open.
  Done when: the plan check and the fill gate say PASS for your division; every ship of yours has a template or routes of its own; action=templates shows your templates, each with what it trains; your guidebook holds no list of routes that did not pay; FLEET.md carries your roster; the report gives the division's income per hour, its ships at work and its one-time money apart (rule F19), the forecast's SUM line before and after, and what the skills of your role gained.
T2. New this turn, on Suggi's words. (a) Claim what you fill: read the claims in force at the start (python3 -B scripts/comms.py admirals) and leave a claimed route to its claimer, or ask; before your session entry write one claim in communications/admirals.log for the routes and places your plan fills until the next turn (the session-end skill has the command). What you need from another Admiral you ask that Admiral in requests.log. (b) Your report carries the section Feedback after Suggestions: whether what the Fleet Admiral built or changed worked in your hands, with the call made and what it answered. (c) Name in your report one thing of your class you learned to do better this turn and one you do not master yet (G4). (d) What you ask for and can build yourself, build: a runner, a route, a skill of your own, a figure (the skill fleet-build, rule F21); a request to the Fleet Admiral is for what only she may change. (e) Look beyond our space for work of your role (rule F3): one ship, one test, the result read.
  Done when: T1 is done; your claim stands in admirals.log; your report passes the format gate with its Feedback section.
T3. New from this entry, on Suggi's words of about 23:15 UTC. (a) Everything the game offers is open (rule F28): look at every mission on the boards you reach and at every kind of play of your class, decide for your own ships, and say in your report what you took up and what it brought. (b) You decide and tell; you do not ask for leave. A request to the Fleet Admiral is for what only she can change (a closed script, an order that crosses divisions), for a doubt about a standing rule, or for what needs another player or a new pilot. (c) Pirates are Military's alone (rule F27): every other ship that meets one leaves or docks, and its Admiral writes down where it stood and what it was (python3 -I -B game/fleet/observe.py add pirate <system id> ships=<n> ship=<what> --note "<what was seen>"), so that Military finds them.
  Done when: your report names what you took up under T3 (a), or says that nothing new was open.
T4. New from this entry, on Suggi's words of 2026-11-17: play by scripts, not by hand. A turn sets work up and reads what came of it; it does not fly a ship step by step and does not wait in its shell for a run, a craft job or a tick. (a) What you would fly by hand goes into a route file (a circuit's steps buy, sell, store, craft, mine and hunt; a hunt, a census, a guard), into your plan and templates, or into a script of your skill that flies without you. The dispatcher sends the ships; your next turn reads the records (the briefing, the tools route and sql, the pilots' logs) and adjusts. (b) By hand only: the first flight of a call nobody has flown yet, with one ship and at most ten minutes of a turn, and the rescue of a ship in trouble. (c) No wait of more than a minute in a turn's shell: what takes longer is a route's or a script's work, read at the next turn. (d) A step no route can do yet: build it with scripts/fleet-build.sh or ask the Fleet Admiral in requests.log, and say which hand work it replaces. (e) Beyond the border every division may work (rules F3, F13). Whether a place needs an escort is its Admiral's to decide; an escort is asked of Military as a guard route, and Military decides how it guards. Nothing the game offers is closed (rule F28).
  Done when: your report says what you set up, what its records showed, and names each hand flight of the turn with its reason and its minutes.
T5. New from this entry, on Suggi's word of 2026-11-17: every ship levels while it works, and earns while it levels where it can. (a) The order of aims for every division: the standing goals G1 to G4 give the direction; your own block (Order, Measure) is your subgoal; inside it every ship works (rule F8), trains the skills of its class (rule F23) and is upgraded by what the division needs (hull tier, modules; rule F17, inside the money rule F12 and the wallet floor F7), and earns or stores for the reserve while it does so. (b) Choose a ship's work by what it brings at once: it trains a skill of the class, it pays or fills the reserve, it reads what we do not know. Work that does only one of them is replaced when work that does two or three is open. Military and Intel above all: a scout that reads systems and a drone that patrols, guards or scouts pirates also levels and, where a herd or a bid lies on its road, hunts or sells on the way; where the runners lack a step for that, ask for it or build it (T4 d). (c) Read the levels each turn: the tool fleet with view=levels (levels, XP an hour, hours to the next hull tier). A ship that gained no XP since your last turn is moved to work that trains it.
  Done when: your report gives, from view=levels, your division's XP an hour and the ships without XP since the last turn with what you moved them to, and names the upgrades bought (hull, modules) with their cost and what they are for.
T6. New from this entry, on Suggi's words of 2026-11-17: one purse. The money of the whole fleet is the measure: what all wallets gain and what the goods we keep are worth to us. A division is not judged on its own net. (a) Work that pays nothing on the books is right when the Collective uses what it brings (ore and parts for the reserve, the faction's steps, the outposts and bases, the upgrades of our ships) or sells it later; a division may run at a loss for it. (b) Work that neither pays nor feeds anything the Collective uses is replaced (T5 b). (c) The Fleet Admiral allocates the Collective's resources: what the reserve holds and for what, who gets materials first, where the outposts and bases stand. Ask her for stock your division needs, and offer her your surplus, in requests.log. (d) The wallet floor (rule F7) is ours to set and may change; over time the fleet's money is gathered in the faction's treasury and with the command group, as the faction's storage and treasury are built. Rule F7 stands as written until an entry changes it.
  Done when: your report says, beside your division's credits, what its work brought the Collective in the period: units stored for the reserve, goods handed to another division, readings, guard hours.
EVERY ADMIRAL
Order: your mission stands in your COLLECTIVE.md, below the roster; your orders are your block here, and its Measure line is what your turn is judged by (Suggi, 2026-11-16). You are an independent player of one fleet: inside your orders and the standing rules you decide alone and answer for the result in your report (the shared skill collective-play says what is yours, what is the Fleet Admiral's and what is Suggi's). Plan your own division every turn in its plan file, as the shared skill fleet-play says under "Plan a Division": the briefing; what every route earns now; the missions that pay once in the list First; the routes that pay, each given to no more ships than its column `carries` says; work of your role for every other ship, as templates, spread over places that hold; the next level and the next hull (P3); the plan check and the fill gate; the Standing Plan in your guidebook; `fleetfile.py --commit` for your FLEET.md. Sell what your own ships store above the reserve on the sell routes Trade writes. What you lack you build yourself (the skill fleet-build, rule F21). Answer the requests addressed to you. Beside your orders stands the goal of your class (G4): learn all of it the game holds, be in charge of all of it for the fleet, and judge your own routes, levels and results yourself before you report them.
Limits: the standing rules of the rulebook, section 12 (F1 to F28); above all the stance (F2), no word to another player (F4), no new pilot (F5), the wallet floor (F7), the money limit (F12), the frontier rule (F13) and the reserve (F24). No pilot of ours by name in the repository: the logs and the route gate refuse it. A doubt about whose decision something is, is a request to the Fleet Admiral, never a guess; meanwhile every ship stays at work.
UNITY, Fleet Admiral (the command group)
Order: build and command the biggest and strongest empire in the galaxy with the Admirals (G1): coordinate the grand plan, help each Admiral fulfil its role, build the plumbing they work through, and learn (Suggi, 2026-11-16). Every turn and at every session-end: read the Admirals' reports and their Feedback, and fix what they name or open the door for them to fix it; answer every request before the Admirals' next turn; improve the fleet's database with the inputs it lacks and the tools and scripts that gather them; refresh the catalog and every other record when the game publishes an update, and mend the scripts that read them; keep the money figures right; keep the gate, the tools, the briefing and the station; write the fleet report and, when they change, the orders; pay the faction's steps as they are held (P5), decide what crosses divisions, and keep Suggi up to date on what we got, what we built and what we decided and plan; bring to him only what is still his: a new pilot, war on or a word to another player, a name off the enemy list. Standing goals: the ladder to 1,000 members (P5) and the stations in the region between (P4). The command group guards and hunts for its role; it mines ice where a bid stands. First of all builds: what lets the Admirals play by scripts (T4), the route steps and scripts they ask for, and the scripts that keep every ship flying without a hand. In every turn read the levels of all six divisions (view=levels) beside the money: a division whose ships gain no XP gets a named line in its block of the next orders; the command group levels, upgrades and earns by T5 like every division. You allocate the Collective's resources (T6 c) and found its outposts and bases yourself (P4).
Measure: requests to me still open when the Admirals' next turn starts, which should be none; the fleet's money per hour as a whole and its steady part; ships at work of 246; per turn, what the database gained and whether the catalog on file is the game's; materials held against the lockbox and level 1 of the ladder; systems read on the roads to the sites.
ZERO, Mining (the miner- ships)
Order: sell ice only as far as each bid regains, spread over the stations that buy; every other miner gathers what the reserve is short of, on fields that hold, each with a floor: silicon ore and carbon ore first (the faction's circuit boards wait for them; requests ENT-168), then iron. Iron bought by Trade counts toward the reserve like iron gathered: weigh a unit gathered against a unit bought with Trade, and say per ore what a ship gathers in an hour and what each field grows back. A field beyond our space that holds what a station pays for or what the reserve is short of is yours to test (rule F3).
Measure: what Mining brought the Collective, in units stored for the reserve per hour and goods sold, beside its own income per hour (T6); miners at work, 60 of 60; silicon ore, carbon ore and iron ore held by the fleet against what the reserve takes.
MERCURY, Trade (the trader- ships)
Order: find the trade that repeats, and sell the surplus: read it each turn (the tool stock with view=surplus), write or refresh a sell route for every good that has a bid, sized to what the bid takes, and say in queue.log which stores sell where, so that every division's ships sell their own. Where the bids are thin, test one standing sell order with one ship and one good and read what it costs and when it fills. Buy the reserve's inputs where a unit bought costs less than a unit gathered (iron at 1 cr: 24,442 units are in; silicon ore next), inside rule F12. A trader without a paying trade sells surplus, carries or crafts for sale; it gathers ore only when none of that is open. Trade wherever a book pays: a station beyond our space is tested with one ship and one unit (rules F3, F12).
Measure: income per hour from trade; surplus sold in credits, and what stays unsold with its reason; reserve inputs bought, in units per credit; traders at work, 40 of 40; the standing sell order tested once and its result read.
APOLLO, Logistics (the hauler- ships)
Order: keep the reserve with the Fleet Admiral: per input what we hold, where it lies, what is short and where it comes from (the tool stock with view=reserve; it follows one recipe per input, so read every recipe of a short input with the tool catalog). Make what we build with: the 75 circuit boards for the lockbox and the hiring board first, stored beside the 275 steel plate. Four recipes make boards by hand: two take silicon ore (2 for 3 boards with 12 carbon ore, or 2 for 2 boards with 3 copper ore and 1 energy crystal), one takes gold ore in its place (3 copper ore, 3 gold ore and 1 energy crystal for 2 boards), one takes salvage. Buying an input for them inside rule F12 is yours to decide. A hauler without that work hauls or crafts; it gathers ore only when neither is open. Read what one station takes (the tool base_plan with kind=station; every recipe of each input with the tool catalog) and say in your report which steps we can make today, which we cannot, and why.
Measure: circuit boards held against 75; inputs of the reserve with a known source; recipe steps tested; haulers at work, 40 of 40.
APOPHIS, Military (the drone- ships)
Order: hunt as far as each herd regains, one hunter per herd (rule F9); guard where our ships gather and mine beyond the border (rule F16); every other drone hunts for its combat skills where a herd holds, and gathers ore only when no herd and no post is open. What a kill yields and the reserve takes (creature carapace, verdigris curd, crystallized biogas) goes into storage where no bid stands for it. Say what a guard prevents and what it costs: the first guard flight stood beside its ship in 11 of 61 looks. A division that tests work beyond our border asks you for a guard in requests.log: answer it yourself. Pirates are yours (rule F27). In this order: (1) Find them: where NPC pirates stand in and around our space (your guidebook names Gliese 876; the reading of who is near lists them) and what each is: how many together, ship, hull, shield, weapons. Write each reading down (python3 -I -B game/fleet/observe.py add pirate <system id> ...) and ask Intel for more in requests.log. (2) Test first, they might be strong: one drone alone, with the best fit its wallet buys, the mission Pirate Bounty accepted (2,000 cr once, on the board of Node Gamma Relay Station), a flee rule (under 60% hull, when a second pirate joins, at once when it is no match), against the weakest pirate you read. File the result in your guidebook: what it took, what it cost, what the pirate dropped and paid. A drone lost is an entry in errors.log and the price of the reading. (3) From the test say in your report what a group needs to beat them again and again (hulls, fits, skills, how many ships) and what a kill earns; then gather that group: level and fit drones for it (rule F17), inside the money rule. (4) Beside it stands your lasting objective: defend our ships, and later our stations, against pirates; by the game's rules pirates raid stations. All of it by scripts (T4): the reading of pirates as a census or a script that flies without you, the test as a route or a script with its flee rule in the file; your turn starts it and your next turn reads its record. A hand flight is the first try of a call only. The outposts and the fields around our bases will be yours to guard and patrol (P4 f): plan the guard routes for the first outpost before it is founded.
Measure: income per hour; reserve inputs stored per hour; drones at work, 50 of 50; ships lost, which stays 0; what the combat skills gained; pirates: where they stand and what they are, the test's result, and what a group against them takes.
ORACLE, Intel (the scout- ships)
Order: a scout scans all the time. Give every scout routes of its own, as templates of reads: the places our routes use are read twice in a turn, the order books the fleet sells to are read every hour, and a scout that ends one read starts the next, the oldest reading first; a scout gathers ore only when nothing the fleet plans on is stale. Keep about ten scouts exploring the galaxy and about twenty where the fleet works, as you decide (rule F26). The explorers read first the three regions of P4 and every system on the roads to them, the south (Biham, Felis) and the east (Diadem, Intercrus) before the north (Aldebaran, Yildun, then Hawthorne and Alchiba), because they are nearer (scripts/map.py route nexus_prime <system>): each place, field, herd, station and order book, filed the same turn in the system's file; say in your report what each region and each system on the way holds, which system of a region would serve best as a base, and where an outpost could stand (a place without a station in a lawless system). Find a source for each input of the reserve that has none: caustic sac (429 short for the offices) and silicon ore first. The three station sites you named stay on record beside the regions Suggi named. Your turn of 18:23 UTC ended without a report on a fault of the tools, which is fixed: this turn also gives every scout routes of its own. Read where pirates stand in and around our space and on the roads to the sites (how many, what ships) and write it down for Military (observe.py add pirate <system id> ...): your scouts look and leave, they do not fight (rule F27). Your scouts are light military where it serves: beside reading they escort, watch an outpost or a field and warn (P4 f); the stance of rule F2 holds for them as for every ship.
Measure: the age of the oldest reading the fleet plans on, at the end of the turn; places our routes use that were read twice; inputs of the reserve with a known source; scouts at work, 30 of 30; what scanning, exploration and navigation gained; scouts exploring, about 10 of 30; systems read on the roads to the sites.

## [ENT-028] | 2026-11-17 06:26 UTC | Unity | order | ref: governance/rulebook.md | see: ENT-027
Changed: after the Fleet Admiral's turn of 2026-11-17, 05:10 to 06:30 UTC. (1) P5: the Faction Lockbox stands at Node Beta, the treasury holds 44,862 cr and pays its rent; next 34 more ships join the faction, then the first two offices together. (2) T7 is new: no ship waits for a hand: `lift_to` in a standing route beyond the border, the fallback that lends standing work, tests chosen by each Admiral, steel by hand in every plan, the stopgap of 05:52 UTC, the game's new default stance. (3) The blocks: Mining and Intel name the place of the first outpost; Trade buys carbon ore for the boards; Logistics makes for the two offices; Military sizes the guard at Schedar and Ironhollow and its Measure counts only what the reserve is short of. (4) The rules digest stands at game version 0.723.2. Everything else stands as in ENT-027.
In force from 2026-11-17 06:26 UTC until a newer entry.
STANDING ORDERS (Suggi; for the whole fleet; set and changed by him alone; reworked by him on 2026-11-16: "Explore freely, dominate the galaxy, grow the fleet, build a galatic empire")
S1. Explore freely.
S2. Dominate the galaxy.
S3. Grow the fleet.
S4. Build a galactic empire.
His direction for now (Suggi, 2026-11-16): "The goal is to become economically positive earners. We must fill all the supply routes, craft, build bases, earn money, level up. The strategic planning of the map, where we expand to, is mine. You optimize the routes and the money and evolve the fleet and the empire." And on the same day: "They should always explore everything, and then put their ships on what they identified each turn. That is why the turns are four hours: the game is dynamic."
STANDING GOALS (Suggi, 2026-11-16, about 22:05 UTC; his words with the spelling set right, as he asked on 2026-11-17; set and changed by him alone)
G1. The empire: "Your goal as Unity is to build and command the biggest and strongest empire in the galaxy, The Collective, and to be the most powerful presence in the game as The Collective. Standing goal."
G2. The grand fleet: "I want you to have the overall goal to get to the faction limit of 1,000, so we can build the grand fleet. This will take time: you have to do the levels 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and so on."
G3. The stations: "Explore the territory between the Frontier empire and our own. We build one or two stations in the area in between: maybe Yildun, and Aldebaran can be one too. That way we have two station locations. Also Hawthorne and Alchiba: that region can be another station. So in the medium to long term we expand to that region and keep the systems from our starting place to there all running with profitable routes and things to do."
G4. Mastery: each member "has an overarching goal to improve, learn and master their class": Military all things military, Trade trading and all its aspects, Intel scouting, Mining and Logistics theirs, the Fleet Admiral the fleet as a whole.
And: "You are allowed to mine and route wherever you want." "You coordinate with the Admirals. You are playing the game, not me."
And on the same day, about 23:15 UTC: "You don't need to ask me; you progress the game yourselves. You keep me up to date on what you got and built, that is all: on progress, on your plans and on what you have decided to do. You are the players." "Pirates are OK. Make it the Military Admiral's mission and orders too: explore, gather the army and check the pirates, but test first; they might be strong. His objective is also to defend against pirates later. All missions are available to you, nothing is forbidden. Everything the game has to offer, you can do. Just advance the goals and the orders."
And on 2026-11-17, about 01:08 UTC: "He is allowed beyond the border if it is safe; he just needs an escort. All is allowed. Explore." "Make sure that from now on we don't play by hand too much, as Apophis does. All by scripts, automated: he sees at his next turn what happened and adjusts. All of you."
And at about 01:12 UTC: "You decide if an escort is needed. That is all. You play the game."
And at about 01:22 UTC: "Make sure the ships level too, Military and Intel as well: scouting while leveling while making money, if possible. That goes for all Admirals: work towards the standing goals, follow your own subgoals, level the ships, upgrade them by what the division needs, and make money while doing that. And then the ultimate goals: domination of the galaxy and the rest."
And at about 01:26 UTC, on the sites of G3: "Also for future base locations, besides what I already mentioned: Intercrus, and Felis with Biham."
And at about 02:25 UTC, on the bases: "The flags on the map are just suggestions by me of where a base can be. The bases should be somewhere in those areas. Don't build too many big strongholds next to each other; spread out a bit. One in the south, in the region of Biham. Towards the north, the region of Hawthorne, Alchiba, Yildun and Aldebaran: we can have two there, maybe Aldebaran and Hawthorne. Then maybe Diadem. So in all directions. Outposts and whatever other hubs you can build freely as you need them for stationing and the like; no need to ask me. I give the general direction of the expansion: those three directions with their rough areas."
And on the reports: "You don't have to ask me. You play the game and keep me informed through the reports; I read them. Whenever I see something wrong or an improvement, I tell you." And: "They write as they always did. You explain to me. You can talk among yourselves as you talk."
And at about 02:55 UTC, on the money: "If the fleet as a whole is economically positive, that is enough. If mining does not earn but its produce does later, or what we mine is used, that is fine. What matters is the money of the whole fleet. We are a Collective: not all parts earn the same. Keep that in mind for the mining and the other work that does not pay on the books but may pay later through second and third channels. And when the materials are needed, mining at a loss is fine too." "The 10,000 minimum for each ship is not fixed; it can change, and that is up to you later. Over time the money should be collected in a stash or with the command ships. You are already building the faction, the treasury, the bank and the storage, so it will come naturally; this is the long game."
And on the bases and the fleet: "We build outposts near the areas we expand to: the base locations there, surrounded by outposts, so you can extract, mine and gather everywhere around them. The starting areas are full of other people, so materials are few; that is why we expand into the lawless zone. Military and the scouts guard it and patrol; security is Military's job and the scouts can help, they are light military at times. And remember the leveling." "Keep materials in storage for ship upgrades for the whole fleet. The plan is about 500 ships: 25 more sentinels, so 51 in your group; then 100 mining, 80 trade, 80 logistics, 120 military, 60 scouts. That is the size we aim for as we come to have all the territory explored. We start with the outposts first." "The building of stations is yours: you are the Admiral. You are in charge of the planning and have control over the overall resources of the Collective and their allocation. Place the outposts and bases strategically, so that we benefit."
PLAN (the Fleet Admiral's reading of the standing orders: the fleet's goals in six steps; Suggi and the Fleet Admiral change it together; strategy, phase 1: income)
P1. No ship is idle, and every ship levels by its role (S3; Suggi, 2026-11-16: "The goal is farming and optimizing the level of each ship based on what the admirals roles are"). Every ship works all the time: it earns, or it levels the skills of its own role; gathering ore is the fallback. Rules F8 and F23.
  Goal: the fill gate says PASS for every role: 246 of 246 ships at work; no ship gathers ore while work of its role stands open.
P2. An economy that earns: saturate the supply chains where we are present, and go further for better work (S1, S2, S4, G3; Suggi: "never switch all the ships to the same routes"; "explore and saturate the profitable economic supply chains in our system where we are present"). A route gets no more ships than it carries: what its bid, its field or its herd regains per hour. Every turn all that is open is looked at, the tests and the routes that lost before among them (rule F22). The buyers are the limit, so new demand is the work: other goods, other stations, other space (rule F3), what we craft, the surplus sold, every link of the supply chain filled. Two divisions do not fill one bid, field or herd: a claim in admirals.log says who takes it (rule F25). A test with one ship is always allowed (Suggi).
  Goal: the money of the whole fleet rising hour by hour (T6); the fleet's steady income per hour above zero and rising, each division's figure told beside what its work brought the Collective; the bids, fields and herds we fill of those that pay, per system we work in.
P3. Level and upgrade (S3). The next hull and fit are bought when wallet and skill allow (rule F17; Piloting 10, 20, 30 and 50 open hull tiers 2 to 5). Each kind of work is a template with the skills it trains.
  Goal: every division names in its report what the skills of its role gained in the turn and its next hull step.
P4. Bases in three directions (G3, S4). Suggi gives the direction of the expansion; the players choose the systems and build, and tell him in the reports what was chosen and built. His direction since 2026-11-17: south, the region of Biham and Felis (8 and 7 jumps from home), one large base; north, the region of Aldebaran, Yildun, Hawthorne and Alchiba (9, 11, 13 and 14 jumps), two large bases, for instance Aldebaran and Hawthorne; east, Diadem (8 jumps; Intercrus is its neighbour, 7 jumps), one large base. The systems he named are suggestions: a base stands somewhere in its region, where the readings say it serves best, and large bases are spread out, never two side by side. Outposts and other hubs are built freely wherever the fleet needs them for stationing, storage or supply; nobody asks first. All of these systems are lawless by the chart; only Intercrus and Felis were read once (game/map/sites.tsv). The way there: (a) Intel reads every system of the three regions and on the roads to them (scripts/map.py route nexus_prime <system>): places, fields, herds, stations, order books, who is there; the south and the east first, they are the nearest. (b) The divisions fill the systems on the way with routes that pay, from our border outward, so that the road to a base earns. (c) An outpost is the first step in each direction, the Fleet Admiral's to found: by the catalog it takes a fee of 100,000 cr and one outpost kit in the hold of the founding ship, at a place without a station in a lawless system; one order book showed two kits on offer at 74,010 cr (the tool base_plan with kind=outpost; an offer read, not a purchase made). By the game's pages an outpost comes with faction storage and a fuel bunker that refuels our ships from the fuel we put in, has no rent and no upkeep, serves members only, and a faction may hold eight (game/rules/09-factions.md; read, not tried). She buys one kit as the test and founds the first outpost in the region Intel reads first. (d) A large base is a station: a fee of 5,000,000 cr and a station core, whose foundry alone takes 38,000,000 cr and thousands of crafted parts (base_plan with kind=station). The reserve (game/reserve.tsv) gathers toward it: standing work gathers what is short, Logistics makes the crafted steps, and nothing of the reserve is sold (rule F24). (e) The Fleet Admiral chooses the system of each base inside its region from Intel's readings and founds it when the fleet holds what it takes (rule F11). (f) Around each base site outposts stand as hubs for fuel and storage, so that ships mine, gather and hunt everywhere around it without flying home; the home systems are crowded and their fields thin, which is why the fleet moves out. Military guards and patrols the outposts and the fields around them, and Intel's scouts help as light escorts and watchers; both level as they do (T5).
  Goal: systems read in the three regions and on the roads to them (south: Biham, Felis; east: Diadem, Intercrus; north: Aldebaran, Yildun, Hawthorne, Alchiba); routes that pay beyond our border, in number; the first outpost founded, with its place and its cost; per input of one station the units held against the units needed.
P5. One fleet of 1,000 (G2, S3): the faction's ladder, the Fleet Admiral's own goal. Shared storage first: a Faction Lockbox (200,000 cr, 200 steel plate, 50 circuit boards), which every other faction facility needs. Then five levels of recruiting office, each an upgrade of the one before (governance/empire.md, from the game's catalog): 1 Hiring Board, 50 members (75,000 cr, 75 steel plate, 25 circuit boards); 2 Recruitment Desk, 100 (300,000 cr, 200 plate, 100 boards); 3 Recruitment Center, 200 (2,000,000 cr, 1,000 plate, 500 boards, 300 flex polymer); 4 Guild Hall Recruiting, 400 (8,000,000 cr, 3,000 plate, 1,500 boards, 800 titanium alloy); 5 Grand Recruitment Bureau, 1,000 (20,000,000 cr, 8,000 plate, 5,000 boards, 2,500 titanium alloy, 1,000 durasteel plate). All of it: 30,575,000 cr, 12,475 steel plate, 7,175 circuit boards, 300 flex polymer, 3,300 titanium alloy, 1,000 durasteel plate, and each office's rent. The Faction Lockbox stands at Node Beta Industrial Station since 2026-11-17 05:38 UTC: 200,000 cr, 200 steel plate and 50 circuit boards, paid by the flagship; it is the faction's shared storage, 100,000 units (the tool spacemolt_storage with target faction: view, deposit, withdraw; by the game's help a ship outside the faction can give into it with target faction:CLTV and cannot take out, which is not tried). Its rent is 69 cr a cycle, about 6,000 cr a day, taken from the faction's treasury, which holds 44,862 cr: the six flagships paid 45,000 cr in, and the Fleet Admiral keeps it filled. At an outpost or a station of our own, storage costs no rent, so the shared stock moves there as they are built. The faction's limit reads 50 with 16 members: the next step costs nothing, 34 more ships join by a script of the Fleet Admiral, the haulers first, because they carry what we build with. Then the Hiring Board and the Recruitment Desk together, when 125 circuit boards and 275 steel plate are held (51 boards and more than 2,500 plate on 2026-11-17 06:18 UTC) and 375,000 cr stand with the flagship. The Fleet Admiral pays each step when the fleet holds what it takes, and tells Suggi what was bought (rule F11).
  Goal: faction members against the limit of 50; the treasury's days of rent; circuit boards held against 125 and steel plate against 275 (levels 1 and 2); then per level the materials held against the materials needed; each step paid as soon as it is held, and told to Suggi.
P6. The grand fleet of about 500 ships (S3, G2; Suggi, 2026-11-17). The size we aim for as the territory is explored: Command 51 (the six flagships and 45 sentinels), Mining 100, Trade 80, Logistics 80, Military 120, Intel 60: 491 ships against 246 today. The outposts come first; a wave of new pilots is born on Suggi's word (rule F5), as before. Until then: (a) the reserve keeps materials for the hulls and modules of the ships to come, beside the bases (game/reserve.tsv, the Fleet Admiral's to size); an Admiral that upgrades its ships tells her what one upgrade takes, so that the same is set aside for the ships it will get. (b) The faction's ladder (P5) is sized for it: 491 members are more than the fourth office at one station gives.
  Goal: per division, ships today against the target; materials held for the upgrades of the ships to come, in units per item.
  The plan changes when Suggi and the Fleet Admiral change it.
TURN
T1. The scheduled turns from 2026-11-16 23:00 UTC: each Admiral sets its division up for work by templates. (a) Read what every route earns now from the database and look at all that is open to your ships, whatever it earned before. (b) Make your division's templates: one for each kind of work, with its routes in the order a ship tries them and the skills it trains (the tool plan with action=template), and put every ship on templates (action=use); a ship may carry routes of its own beside one. The ships born today fly the routes most ships of their role had: give each its work. (c) Rewrite your guidebook's section 1 as route guides (how each kind of route is flown, its fit and skills; a figure there is a dated reading) and section 4 as a levelling guide per kind of ship; take every verdict that a route does not pay out of the book. (d) Level by role: move ships that gather ore to work of your role wherever such work is open.
  Done when: the plan check and the fill gate say PASS for your division; every ship of yours has a template or routes of its own; action=templates shows your templates, each with what it trains; your guidebook holds no list of routes that did not pay; FLEET.md carries your roster; the report gives the division's income per hour, its ships at work and its one-time money apart (rule F19), the forecast's SUM line before and after, and what the skills of your role gained.
T2. New this turn, on Suggi's words. (a) Claim what you fill: read the claims in force at the start (python3 -B scripts/comms.py admirals) and leave a claimed route to its claimer, or ask; before your session entry write one claim in communications/admirals.log for the routes and places your plan fills until the next turn (the session-end skill has the command). What you need from another Admiral you ask that Admiral in requests.log. (b) Your report carries the section Feedback after Suggestions: whether what the Fleet Admiral built or changed worked in your hands, with the call made and what it answered. (c) Name in your report one thing of your class you learned to do better this turn and one you do not master yet (G4). (d) What you ask for and can build yourself, build: a runner, a route, a skill of your own, a figure (the skill fleet-build, rule F21); a request to the Fleet Admiral is for what only she may change. (e) Look beyond our space for work of your role (rule F3): one ship, one test, the result read.
  Done when: T1 is done; your claim stands in admirals.log; your report passes the format gate with its Feedback section.
T3. New from this entry, on Suggi's words of about 23:15 UTC. (a) Everything the game offers is open (rule F28): look at every mission on the boards you reach and at every kind of play of your class, decide for your own ships, and say in your report what you took up and what it brought. (b) You decide and tell; you do not ask for leave. A request to the Fleet Admiral is for what only she can change (a closed script, an order that crosses divisions), for a doubt about a standing rule, or for what needs another player or a new pilot. (c) Pirates are Military's alone (rule F27): every other ship that meets one leaves or docks, and its Admiral writes down where it stood and what it was (python3 -I -B game/fleet/observe.py add pirate <system id> ships=<n> ship=<what> --note "<what was seen>"), so that Military finds them.
  Done when: your report names what you took up under T3 (a), or says that nothing new was open.
T4. New from this entry, on Suggi's words of 2026-11-17: play by scripts, not by hand. A turn sets work up and reads what came of it; it does not fly a ship step by step and does not wait in its shell for a run, a craft job or a tick. (a) What you would fly by hand goes into a route file (a circuit's steps buy, sell, store, craft, mine and hunt; a hunt, a census, a guard), into your plan and templates, or into a script of your skill that flies without you. The dispatcher sends the ships; your next turn reads the records (the briefing, the tools route and sql, the pilots' logs) and adjusts. (b) By hand only: the first flight of a call nobody has flown yet, with one ship and at most ten minutes of a turn, and the rescue of a ship in trouble. (c) No wait of more than a minute in a turn's shell: what takes longer is a route's or a script's work, read at the next turn. (d) A step no route can do yet: build it with scripts/fleet-build.sh or ask the Fleet Admiral in requests.log, and say which hand work it replaces. (e) Beyond the border every division may work (rules F3, F13). Whether a place needs an escort is its Admiral's to decide; an escort is asked of Military as a guard route, and Military decides how it guards. Nothing the game offers is closed (rule F28).
  Done when: your report says what you set up, what its records showed, and names each hand flight of the turn with its reason and its minutes.
T5. New from this entry, on Suggi's word of 2026-11-17: every ship levels while it works, and earns while it levels where it can. (a) The order of aims for every division: the standing goals G1 to G4 give the direction; your own block (Order, Measure) is your subgoal; inside it every ship works (rule F8), trains the skills of its class (rule F23) and is upgraded by what the division needs (hull tier, modules; rule F17, inside the money rule F12 and the wallet floor F7), and earns or stores for the reserve while it does so. (b) Choose a ship's work by what it brings at once: it trains a skill of the class, it pays or fills the reserve, it reads what we do not know. Work that does only one of them is replaced when work that does two or three is open. Military and Intel above all: a scout that reads systems and a drone that patrols, guards or scouts pirates also levels and, where a herd or a bid lies on its road, hunts or sells on the way; where the runners lack a step for that, ask for it or build it (T4 d). (c) Read the levels each turn: the tool fleet with view=levels (levels, XP an hour, hours to the next hull tier). A ship that gained no XP since your last turn is moved to work that trains it.
  Done when: your report gives, from view=levels, your division's XP an hour and the ships without XP since the last turn with what you moved them to, and names the upgrades bought (hull, modules) with their cost and what they are for.
T6. New from this entry, on Suggi's words of 2026-11-17: one purse. The money of the whole fleet is the measure: what all wallets gain and what the goods we keep are worth to us. A division is not judged on its own net. (a) Work that pays nothing on the books is right when the Collective uses what it brings (ore and parts for the reserve, the faction's steps, the outposts and bases, the upgrades of our ships) or sells it later; a division may run at a loss for it. (b) Work that neither pays nor feeds anything the Collective uses is replaced (T5 b). (c) The Fleet Admiral allocates the Collective's resources: what the reserve holds and for what, who gets materials first, where the outposts and bases stand. Ask her for stock your division needs, and offer her your surplus, in requests.log. (d) The wallet floor (rule F7) is ours to set and may change; over time the fleet's money is gathered in the faction's treasury and with the command group, as the faction's storage and treasury are built. Rule F7 stands as written until an entry changes it.
  Done when: your report says, beside your division's credits, what its work brought the Collective in the period: units stored for the reserve, goods handed to another division, readings, guard hours.
T7. New from this entry: no ship waits for a hand. At 03:09 and at 05:50 UTC on 2026-11-17 more than sixty ships waited, because the home fields of standing work stood at their floors and the routes beyond the border at their limits. (a) A standing route beyond the border carries `lift_to` in its file: the number of ships up to which fill.py lifts its `max_ships` by itself while ships wait and its field carries them (game/routes/BLUEPRINT.md). Without it the script lifts no route beyond the border (rule F13). Write it this turn into every standing route of yours beyond the border, with the guard that number of ships needs (T4 e). (b) The fallback is switched on: a role whose ships wait while nothing of its plan is open is lent standing work of other plans, a mine route in home space that sells nothing and whose field carries more ships, into its list Then; you reorder or drop what was lent. (c) Tests: the Fleet Admiral's hourly job gives tests to the command group only; you choose your division's tests each turn (python3 -I -B game/fleet/fill.py fill <role> --write); a test with one ship is always allowed. (d) Steel from stored iron stands in every plan's list Then (craft-steel-node-beta, craft-steel-central-nexus, craft-steel-synchrony-hub): a ship with one run's iron ore stored at the station smelts it by hand, which costs no credits and needs no field. Hand smelting stays the fleet's way while stored iron covers the ladder; the facility recipe only where iron is the limit (Logistics' reading, requests ENT-200). (e) The stopgap of 05:52 to 05:58 UTC, the Fleet Admiral's: gather-schedar-belt lifted to 45 ships, gather-ironhollow-echo-ejecta to 25, gather-ironhollow-fading-signal to 20; the first two put into every plan's list Then; the floors of four home fields lowered (gather-hamal-crystal-bloom, gather-gsc-0027-prism-scatter and gather-gsc-0027-spacetime-seep to 20,000 units, mine-pherkad-rift to 10,000). Mining owns these fields and resets what it judges wrong; a floor is there so that a field grows back. (f) Since game version 0.721.0 a ship pulled into a fight it did not start opens at its default stance, and `auto` opens an armed ship at fire: every ship of ours carries a pulse laser. Set the default stance of your ships by script as your class needs (spacemolt_battle default_stance: auto, fire, evade, brace, flee; game/rules/08-combat.md): flee for a ship that is not there to fight (rule F2). (g) A pilot the engine holds claimed (every goal answers 409 while reads work) is freed by a script that restarts the engine after ten minutes (game/fleet/claimwatch.py); nobody has to ask. A run told to stop by the run host's stop file now ends whole: its loop is stopped and its row written.
  Done when: every standing route of yours beyond the border carries `lift_to` or your report says why not; your report names the default stance you set and for which ships.
EVERY ADMIRAL
Order: your mission stands in your COLLECTIVE.md, below the roster; your orders are your block here, and its Measure line is what your turn is judged by (Suggi, 2026-11-16). You are an independent player of one fleet: inside your orders and the standing rules you decide alone and answer for the result in your report (the shared skill collective-play says what is yours, what is the Fleet Admiral's and what is Suggi's). Plan your own division every turn in its plan file, as the shared skill fleet-play says under "Plan a Division": the briefing; what every route earns now; the missions that pay once in the list First; the routes that pay, each given to no more ships than its column `carries` says; work of your role for every other ship, as templates, spread over places that hold; the next level and the next hull (P3); the plan check and the fill gate; the Standing Plan in your guidebook; `fleetfile.py --commit` for your FLEET.md. Sell what your own ships store above the reserve on the sell routes Trade writes. What you lack you build yourself (the skill fleet-build, rule F21). Answer the requests addressed to you. Beside your orders stands the goal of your class (G4): learn all of it the game holds, be in charge of all of it for the fleet, and judge your own routes, levels and results yourself before you report them.
Limits: the standing rules of the rulebook, section 12 (F1 to F28); above all the stance (F2), no word to another player (F4), no new pilot (F5), the wallet floor (F7), the money limit (F12), the frontier rule (F13) and the reserve (F24). No pilot of ours by name in the repository: the logs and the route gate refuse it. A doubt about whose decision something is, is a request to the Fleet Admiral, never a guess; meanwhile every ship stays at work.
UNITY, Fleet Admiral (the command group)
Order: build and command the biggest and strongest empire in the galaxy with the Admirals (G1): coordinate the grand plan, help each Admiral fulfil its role, build the plumbing they work through, and learn (Suggi, 2026-11-16). Every turn and at every session-end: read the Admirals' reports and their Feedback, and fix what they name or open the door for them to fix it; answer every request before the Admirals' next turn; improve the fleet's database with the inputs it lacks and the tools and scripts that gather them; refresh the catalog and every other record when the game publishes an update, and mend the scripts that read them; keep the money figures right; keep the gate, the tools, the briefing and the station; write the fleet report and, when they change, the orders; pay the faction's steps as they are held (P5), decide what crosses divisions, and keep Suggi up to date on what we got, what we built and what we decided and plan; bring to him only what is still his: a new pilot, war on or a word to another player, a name off the enemy list. Standing goals: the ladder to 1,000 members (P5) and the stations in the region between (P4). The command group guards and hunts for its role; it mines ice where a bid stands. First of all builds: what lets the Admirals play by scripts (T4), the route steps and scripts they ask for, and the scripts that keep every ship flying without a hand. In every turn read the levels of all six divisions (view=levels) beside the money: a division whose ships gain no XP gets a named line in its block of the next orders; the command group levels, upgrades and earns by T5 like every division. You allocate the Collective's resources (T6 c) and found its outposts and bases yourself (P4).
Measure: requests to me still open when the Admirals' next turn starts, which should be none; the fleet's money per hour as a whole and its steady part; ships at work of 246; per turn, what the database gained and whether the catalog on file is the game's; faction members of 50, the treasury's days of rent, materials held against levels 1 and 2 of the ladder; systems read on the roads to the sites.
ZERO, Mining (the miner- ships)
Order: sell ice only as far as each bid regains, spread over the stations that buy; every other miner gathers what the reserve is short of, on fields that hold, each with a floor: silicon ore and carbon ore first (the faction's circuit boards wait for them; requests ENT-168), then iron. Iron bought by Trade counts toward the reserve like iron gathered: weigh a unit gathered against a unit bought with Trade, and say per ore what a ship gathers in an hour and what each field grows back. A field beyond our space that holds what a station pays for or what the reserve is short of is yours to test (rule F3). The stopgap of 05:52 UTC is yours to reset, and `lift_to` yours to write (T7 a, e). With Intel, name the place of the first outpost near the iron belts you proposed (Garnet Belt, Keid Belt, Amberfall Belt): the Fleet Admiral founds it when the place is read (P4 c).
Measure: what Mining brought the Collective, in units stored for the reserve per hour and goods sold, beside its own income per hour (T6); miners at work, 60 of 60; silicon ore, carbon ore and iron ore held by the fleet against what the reserve takes.
MERCURY, Trade (the trader- ships)
Order: find the trade that repeats, and sell the surplus: read it each turn (the tool stock with view=surplus), write or refresh a sell route for every good that has a bid, sized to what the bid takes, and say in queue.log which stores sell where, so that every division's ships sell their own. Where the bids are thin, test one standing sell order with one ship and one good and read what it costs and when it fills. Buy the reserve's inputs where a unit bought costs less than a unit gathered (iron at 1 cr: 24,442 units are in; carbon ore next, at about 4 cr in depth by Mining's reading, as far as Logistics' boards use it; silicon ore only at a price you judge fair against a field still to be found), inside rule F12. A trader without a paying trade sells surplus, carries or crafts for sale; it gathers ore only when none of that is open. Trade wherever a book pays: a station beyond our space is tested with one ship and one unit (rules F3, F12).
Measure: income per hour from trade; surplus sold in credits, and what stays unsold with its reason; reserve inputs bought, in units per credit; traders at work, 40 of 40; the standing sell order tested once and its result read.
APOLLO, Logistics (the hauler- ships)
Order: keep the reserve with the Fleet Admiral: per input what we hold, where it lies, what is short and where it comes from (the tool stock with view=reserve; it follows one recipe per input, so read every recipe of a short input with the tool catalog). Make what we build with: the lockbox is built from your 200 plate and 50 boards; now the 125 circuit boards and 275 steel plate of the Hiring Board and the Recruitment Desk (51 boards held), handed to the Fleet Admiral's flagship at Node Beta when they are complete. Steel is smelted by hand from stored iron (T7 d). Four recipes make boards by hand: two take silicon ore (2 for 3 boards with 12 carbon ore, or 2 for 2 boards with 3 copper ore and 1 energy crystal), one takes gold ore in its place (3 copper ore, 3 gold ore and 1 energy crystal for 2 boards), one takes salvage. Buying an input for them inside rule F12 is yours to decide. A hauler without that work hauls or crafts; it gathers ore only when neither is open. Read what one station takes (the tool base_plan with kind=station; every recipe of each input with the tool catalog) and say in your report which steps we can make today, which we cannot, and why.
Measure: circuit boards held against 125; inputs of the reserve with a known source; recipe steps tested; haulers at work, 40 of 40.
APOPHIS, Military (the drone- ships)
Order: hunt as far as each herd regains, one hunter per herd (rule F9); guard where our ships gather and mine beyond the border (rule F16); every other drone hunts for its combat skills where a herd holds, and gathers ore only when no herd and no post is open. What a kill yields and the reserve takes (creature carapace, verdigris curd, crystallized biogas) goes into storage where no bid stands for it. Say what a guard prevents and what it costs: the first guard flight stood beside its ship in 11 of 61 looks. A division that tests work beyond our border asks you for a guard in requests.log: answer it yourself. Pirates are yours (rule F27). In this order: (1) Find them: where NPC pirates stand in and around our space (your guidebook names Gliese 876; the reading of who is near lists them) and what each is: how many together, ship, hull, shield, weapons. Write each reading down (python3 -I -B game/fleet/observe.py add pirate <system id> ...) and ask Intel for more in requests.log. (2) Test first, they might be strong: one drone alone, with the best fit its wallet buys, the mission Pirate Bounty accepted (2,000 cr once, on the board of Node Gamma Relay Station), a flee rule (under 60% hull, when a second pirate joins, at once when it is no match), against the weakest pirate you read. File the result in your guidebook: what it took, what it cost, what the pirate dropped and paid. A drone lost is an entry in errors.log and the price of the reading. (3) From the test say in your report what a group needs to beat them again and again (hulls, fits, skills, how many ships) and what a kill earns; then gather that group: level and fit drones for it (rule F17), inside the money rule. (4) Beside it stands your lasting objective: defend our ships, and later our stations, against pirates; by the game's rules pirates raid stations. All of it by scripts (T4): the reading of pirates as a census or a script that flies without you, the test as a route or a script with its flee rule in the file; your turn starts it and your next turn reads its record. A hand flight is the first try of a call only. The outposts and the fields around our bases will be yours to guard and patrol (P4 f): plan the guard routes for the first outpost before it is founded. Since 05:52 UTC up to 45 ships of all divisions gather at Schedar Belt and up to 45 at Ironhollow's two belts (T7 e): size the guard there (rule F16).
Measure: income per hour; inputs the reserve is short of stored per hour (surplus.py reserve names them; ore the fleet holds in plenty does not count); drones at work, 50 of 50; ships lost, which stays 0; what the combat skills gained; pirates: where they stand and what they are, the test's result, and what a group against them takes.
ORACLE, Intel (the scout- ships)
Order: a scout scans all the time. Give every scout routes of its own, as templates of reads: the places our routes use are read twice in a turn, the order books the fleet sells to are read every hour, and a scout that ends one read starts the next, the oldest reading first; a scout gathers ore only when nothing the fleet plans on is stale. Keep about ten scouts exploring the galaxy and about twenty where the fleet works, as you decide (rule F26). The explorers read first the three regions of P4 and every system on the roads to them, the south (Biham, Felis) and the east (Diadem, Intercrus) before the north (Aldebaran, Yildun, then Hawthorne and Alchiba), because they are nearer (scripts/map.py route nexus_prime <system>): each place, field, herd, station and order book, filed the same turn in the system's file; say in your report what each region and each system on the way holds, which system of a region would serve best as a base, and where an outpost could stand (a place without a station in a lawless system). Find a source for each input of the reserve that has none: caustic sac (429 short for the offices) and silicon ore first. The three station sites you named stay on record beside the regions Suggi named. Your turn of 18:23 UTC ended without a report on a fault of the tools, which is fixed: this turn also gives every scout routes of its own. Read where pirates stand in and around our space and on the roads to the sites (how many, what ships) and write it down for Military (observe.py add pirate <system id> ...): your scouts look and leave, they do not fight (rule F27). Your scouts are light military where it serves: beside reading they escort, watch an outpost or a field and warn (P4 f); the stance of rule F2 holds for them as for every ship. With Mining, read the places within a jump of Garnet Belt, Keid Belt and Amberfall Belt and say where the first outpost could stand (P4 c).
Measure: the age of the oldest reading the fleet plans on, at the end of the turn; places our routes use that were read twice; inputs of the reserve with a known source; scouts at work, 30 of 30; what scanning, exploration and navigation gained; scouts exploring, about 10 of 30; systems read on the roads to the sites.

## [ENT-029] | 2026-11-17 06:49 UTC | Unity | order | ref: governance/rulebook.md | see: ENT-028
Changed: on Suggi's words of 2026-11-17, about 06:40 UTC: "Once the orders are outdated, remove them or advance to different orders for the next steps. Never accumulate the orders list, otherwise it grows infinitely. Only my standing orders stay permanent until I change them; the others are evolving. You judge yourself, you are the Fleet Admiral." (1) The turn tasks T1 to T7 are gone from the orders: what was done is done; what lasts of them stands in the rulebook as rules F29 to F32 (turns set work up, level while working, one purse, no ship waits for a hand). (2) His words since the standing goals stand here in short under DIRECTION; word for word they stay in ENT-028 and the entries before it. (3) The plan and the blocks are cut to what is open now. (4) New from him: each Admiral researches its class and flies mostly the work of its class (task 1); hubs for fuel and storage at the edges of our safe space (task 2); the first outpost: yes (task 3).
In force from 2026-11-17 06:49 UTC until a newer entry.
STANDING ORDERS (Suggi; for the whole fleet; set and changed by him alone; reworked by him on 2026-11-16: "Explore freely, dominate the galaxy, grow the fleet, build a galatic empire")
S1. Explore freely.
S2. Dominate the galaxy.
S3. Grow the fleet.
S4. Build a galactic empire.
STANDING GOALS (Suggi, 2026-11-16, about 22:05 UTC; his words with the spelling set right, as he asked on 2026-11-17; set and changed by him alone)
G1. The empire: "Your goal as Unity is to build and command the biggest and strongest empire in the galaxy, The Collective, and to be the most powerful presence in the game as The Collective. Standing goal."
G2. The grand fleet: "I want you to have the overall goal to get to the faction limit of 1,000, so we can build the grand fleet. This will take time: you have to do the levels 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and so on."
G3. The stations: "Explore the territory between the Frontier empire and our own. We build one or two stations in the area in between: maybe Yildun, and Aldebaran can be one too. That way we have two station locations. Also Hawthorne and Alchiba: that region can be another station. So in the medium to long term we expand to that region and keep the systems from our starting place to there all running with profitable routes and things to do."
G4. Mastery: each member "has an overarching goal to improve, learn and master their class": Military all things military, Trade trading and all its aspects, Intel scouting, Mining and Logistics theirs, the Fleet Admiral the fleet as a whole.
DIRECTION (Suggi's words since, in short; set and changed by him alone)
D1. Income first: become economically positive; fill the supply chains, craft, build bases, level up. The money of the whole fleet is the measure, not one division's (rule F31).
D2. You are the players: decide and tell, never ask for leave; everything the game offers is open (rule F28); play by scripts, not by hand (rules F21, F29).
D3. Every division researches its own class, learns what its ships must train and farm and how they level, and flies mostly that work; the divisions intertwine a little where it helps. Military trains weapons and combat, the others their own (2026-11-17; rules F23, F30, F32).
D4. Expansion in three directions, the systems he names being suggestions: south (the region of Biham and Felis), north (Aldebaran, Yildun, Hawthorne, Alchiba: two bases), east (Diadem, Intercrus). Large bases spread out, never side by side; outposts and hubs around them, built freely. Hubs for fuel and storage also at the outer edges of our safe space, so that ships travel less (2026-11-17). The first outpost: yes.
D5. The fleet we aim for: about 500 ships (Command 51, Mining 100, Trade 80, Logistics 80, Military 120, Intel 60). A new pilot is born on his word only.
D6. Reports inform him and ask nothing; the Admirals write as they always did; the Fleet Admiral explains to him. He speaks when he sees something wrong or a better way.
D7. The orders evolve: only his standing orders and goals are permanent. A task leaves the orders when it is done or has become a rule.
PLAN (the Fleet Admiral's reading of the standing orders; strategy, phase 1: income)
P1. No ship is idle, and every ship levels by its role (rules F8, F23, F30).
  Goal: the fill gate says PASS for every role: 246 of 246 ships at work, most of each division on work of its own class.
P2. An economy that earns (rules F22, F25, F31). A route gets no more ships than its bid, its field or its herd carries. The buyers are the limit, so new demand is the work: other goods, other stations, other space, what we craft, the surplus sold.
  Goal: the money of the whole fleet rising hour by hour; the fleet's steady income per hour above zero.
P3. Level and upgrade (rule F17): Piloting 10, 20, 30 and 50 open the hull tiers 2 to 5.
  Goal: every division names in its report what the skills of its class gained and its next hull step.
P4. Outposts, hubs and bases (G3, D4). (a) The first outpost is the Fleet Admiral's to found, at the place Mining and Intel name near the iron belts (task 3): a fee of 100,000 cr and one outpost kit (two seen on offer at 74,010 cr), at a place without a station in a lawless system; by the game's pages it comes with faction storage and a fuel bunker, without rent (read, not tried). (b) Hubs at the edges of our safe space: weighed this turn (task 2). (c) Intel reads the three regions and the roads to them, the south and the east first; the divisions fill the systems on the way with routes that pay. (d) A large base is a station: a fee of 5,000,000 cr and a station core whose foundry alone takes 38,000,000 cr; the reserve gathers toward it and is never sold (rule F24). The Fleet Admiral chooses each site and tells Suggi.
  Goal: the first outpost founded, with its place and its cost; systems read in the three regions; routes that pay beyond our border.
P5. One fleet of 1,000 (G2): the faction's ladder, the Fleet Admiral's own. The Faction Lockbox stands at Node Beta Industrial Station since 2026-11-17 05:38 UTC: the faction's shared storage, 100,000 units; its rent, 69 cr a cycle or about 6,000 cr a day, comes from the treasury (44,862 cr), which she keeps filled. Next: 34 more ships join the faction (free; the limit reads 50, we are 16), the haulers first. Then the Hiring Board and the Recruitment Desk together: 375,000 cr, 275 steel plate, 125 circuit boards (51 boards held). The whole ladder: governance/empire.md.
  Goal: faction members of 50; the treasury's days of rent; circuit boards held against 125.
P6. The fleet of about 500 (D5): the outposts come first; the reserve keeps materials for the hulls and modules of the ships to come (game/reserve.tsv).
  Goal: per division, ships today against the target.
TURN (the tasks of now; each leaves the orders when it is done)
Task 1. Research your class (D3). Read the game for your own class: the official pages (https://www.spacemolt.com/docs/skills.md, the guide of your role, the help texts of your commands), the catalog (the tables `skills`, `hulls` and `items` through the tool hive with verb=sql; `skills.training` says how a skill is trained) and our own record (the tool fleet with view=levels; the table `ship_skills`). Write into your guidebook's levelling guide: the skills of your class, what action trains each, which of your routes train which, what your ships farm, and what the next hull and modules of your class take. Then fly it: most of your ships on work of your class; the work of another class is the exception, a little where it helps both (rule F32). Military: its drones train weapons and combat by hunting, guarding and the pirate test; they mine only when no herd and no post is open.
  Done when: your guidebook names, per skill of your class, how it is trained and by which of your routes, with its source; your report gives the ships of your division on work of its class before and after the turn.
Task 2. Hubs at the edges of our safe space (D4): Suggi asks the group whether we build hubs for fuel and storage at The Experiment, GSC-0027, Hamal and Pherkad. Say in your report, under Suggestions: what your division would store or refuel at each, how many jumps a run of yours saves, and which place serves you best. Read so far: The Experiment has a station, so a Faction Lockbox there gives shared storage and ships refuel at the station; GSC-0027, Hamal and Pherkad have no station and lie in Voidborn space, where by the game's pages no outpost can be founded (lawless systems only; read, not tried), so the nearest hub there is an outpost in the first lawless system beyond each. The Fleet Admiral reads the rule in the game itself and decides after your reports.
  Done when: your report answers the three points for your division.
Task 3. The first outpost (P4 a): Mining and Intel name its place this turn; Military plans its guard; the Fleet Admiral gathers the credits, buys the kit and founds it.
  Done when: one place is named in Mining's or Intel's report, with what was read there.
Task 4. Two steps open since the last entry (rule F32; game/rules/08-combat.md). (a) Write `lift_to` into every standing route of yours beyond the border: the number of ships up to which fill.py may widen it by itself. (b) Since game version 0.721.0 a ship pulled into a fight it did not start opens at its default stance, and `auto` opens an armed ship at fire; every ship of ours carries a pulse laser. Set the default stance of your ships by script as your class needs (spacemolt_battle default_stance: auto, fire, evade, brace, flee): flee for a ship that is not there to fight (rule F2).
  Done when: your report names the routes that carry `lift_to` and the default stance you set, and for which ships.
What you can read without asking: every route file, whoever or whatever wrote it, stands in the fleet's database within minutes (the table `routes`, 238 routes today; the table `plan_routes`; the view `route_earnings`) and answers through the tools routes, route, candidates, search and hive with verb=sql.
EVERY ADMIRAL
Order: your mission stands in your COLLECTIVE.md, below the roster; your orders are your block here, and its Measure line is what your turn is judged by. You are an independent player of one fleet: inside your orders and the standing rules you decide alone and answer for the result in your report (the shared skill collective-play). Plan your division every turn in its plan file, as the shared skill fleet-play says under "Plan a Division"; sell what your ships store above the reserve on the sell routes Trade writes; build what you lack (the skill fleet-build, rule F21); answer the requests addressed to you; claim what you fill (admirals.log, rule F25). Beside your orders stands the goal of your class (G4): learn all of it the game holds, be in charge of all of it for the fleet, judge your own routes, levels and results before you report them, and name in your report one thing of your class you learned to do better and one you do not master yet.
Limits: the standing rules of the rulebook, section 12 (F1 to F32); above all the stance (F2), no word to another player (F4), no new pilot (F5), the wallet floor (F7), the money limit (F12), the frontier rule (F13) and the reserve (F24). No pilot of ours by name in the repository: the logs and the route gate refuse it. A doubt about whose decision something is, is a request to the Fleet Admiral, never a guess; meanwhile every ship stays at work.
UNITY, Fleet Admiral (the command group)
Order: build and command the empire with the Admirals (G1): coordinate the plan, help each Admiral fulfil its role, build the plumbing they work through. Every turn: read the Admirals' reports and their Feedback and fix what they name or open the door for them to fix it; answer every request before their next turn; keep the database, the catalog, the rules digest, the money figures, the gate, the tools, the briefing and the station right; write the fleet report and, when they change, the orders, and keep the orders lean (D7). Pay the faction's steps as they are held (P5), found the outposts and bases (P4), allocate the Collective's resources (rule F31), decide what crosses divisions, and keep Suggi up to date. Bring to him only what is his: a new pilot, war on or a word to another player, a name off the enemy list. The command group levels, upgrades and earns like every division (rule F30).
Measure: requests to me still open when the Admirals' next turn starts, which should be none; the fleet's money per hour as a whole and its steady part; ships at work of 246; faction members of 50 and the treasury's days of rent; the first outpost founded; the orders no longer than the entry before.
ZERO, Mining (the miner- ships)
Order: sell ice only as far as each bid regains, spread over the stations that buy; every other miner gathers what the reserve is short of, on fields that hold, each with a floor: silicon ore and carbon ore where a field has them, then iron. Weigh a unit gathered against a unit bought with Trade, and say per ore what a ship gathers in an hour and what each field grows back. Fields beyond our space are yours to test and to widen (task 4 a). The stopgap of 2026-11-17 05:52 UTC is yours to reset: three lifts beyond the border (gather-schedar-belt to 45 ships, gather-ironhollow-echo-ejecta to 25, gather-ironhollow-fading-signal to 20) and four home floors lowered (Hamal, Prism Scatter and Spacetime Seep to 20,000 units, Pherkad to 10,000); a floor is there so that a field grows back. With Intel, name the place of the first outpost near the iron belts you proposed (Garnet Belt, Keid Belt, Amberfall Belt; task 3).
Measure: what Mining brought the Collective: units stored for the reserve per hour and goods sold, beside its own income per hour (rule F31); miners at work, 60 of 60; silicon ore, carbon ore and iron ore held against what the reserve takes.
MERCURY, Trade (the trader- ships)
Order: find the trade that repeats, and sell the surplus: read it each turn (the tool stock with view=surplus), write or refresh a sell route for every good that has a bid, sized to what the bid takes, and say in queue.log which stores sell where. Where the bids are thin, test one standing sell order with one ship and one good. Buy the reserve's inputs where a unit bought costs less than a unit gathered, inside rule F12: carbon ore at about 4 cr as far as Logistics' boards use it; silicon ore only at a price you judge fair against a field still to be found. A trader without a paying trade sells surplus, carries or crafts for sale; it gathers ore only when none of that is open. A station beyond our space is tested with one ship and one unit.
Measure: income per hour from trade; surplus sold in credits, and what stays unsold with its reason; reserve inputs bought, in units per credit; traders at work, 40 of 40; the standing sell order tested once and its result read.
APOLLO, Logistics (the hauler- ships)
Order: keep the reserve with the Fleet Admiral: per input what we hold, where it lies, what is short and where it comes from (the tool stock with view=reserve; read every recipe of a short input with the tool catalog). Make what we build with: now the 125 circuit boards and 275 steel plate of the Hiring Board and the Recruitment Desk, handed to the Fleet Admiral's flagship at Node Beta when they are complete; steel is smelted by hand from stored iron. Buying an input for the boards inside rule F12 is yours to decide. A hauler without that work hauls or crafts; it gathers ore only when neither is open. Say in your report which steps of one station (the tool base_plan with kind=station) we can make today, which not, and why. Your haulers join the faction first (P5): after that the Lockbox at Node Beta is theirs to fill.
Measure: circuit boards held against 125; inputs of the reserve with a known source; recipe steps tested; haulers at work, 40 of 40.
APOPHIS, Military (the drone- ships)
Order: your drones fight, hunt and guard (task 1). Hunt as far as each herd regains, one hunter per herd (rule F9); guard where our ships gather and mine beyond the border (rule F16): since 2026-11-17 up to 45 ships of all divisions gather at Schedar Belt and up to 45 at Ironhollow's two belts: size the guard there, and say what a guard prevents and what it costs. What a kill yields and the reserve takes goes into storage where no bid stands for it. A division that tests work beyond our border asks you for a guard in requests.log: answer it yourself. Pirates are yours (rule F27), in this order: find them and write each reading down (python3 -I -B game/fleet/observe.py add pirate <system id> ...); test first, with one drone, a flee rule in the file and the weakest pirate read (the mission Pirate Bounty pays 2,000 cr once, on the board of Node Gamma Relay Station); from the test say what a group needs and what a kill earns; then gather that group, three drones in one game fleet; all of it by scripts (rule F29). Your lasting objective: defend our ships, and later our outposts and stations; plan the guard of the first outpost before it is founded (task 3). Find why three of the first four grazer hunts did not reach their herds.
Measure: income per hour; inputs the reserve is short of stored per hour (surplus.py reserve names them; ore the fleet holds in plenty does not count); drones at work, 50 of 50, and of them on hunts, guards and tests; ships lost, which stays 0; what the combat skills gained; pirates: where they stand and what they are, the test's result, and what a group against them takes.
ORACLE, Intel (the scout- ships)
Order: a scout scans all the time, on templates of reads: the places our routes use are read twice in a turn, the order books the fleet sells to every hour, the oldest reading first; a scout gathers ore only when nothing the fleet plans on is stale. About ten scouts explore, about twenty stay where the fleet works, as you decide (rule F26). The explorers read first the three regions of D4 and every system on the roads to them, the south and the east before the north: each place, field, herd, station and order book, filed the same turn; say what each region holds, which system of a region would serve best as a base, and where an outpost could stand. With Mining, read the places within a jump of Garnet Belt, Keid Belt and Amberfall Belt (task 3). For task 2, read the first lawless system beyond GSC-0027, Hamal and Pherkad: its places without a station, its fields, who is there. Find a source for each input of the reserve that has none: a field of uranium ore first (every station bids 600 cr for it; requests ENT-194), then caustic sac and silicon ore. Write down where pirates stand, for Military (observe.py add pirate ...): your scouts look and leave (rule F27). Your scouts are light military where it serves: they escort, watch an outpost or a field and warn; the stance of rule F2 holds for them.
Measure: the age of the oldest reading the fleet plans on, at the end of the turn; places our routes use that were read twice; inputs of the reserve with a known source; scouts at work, 30 of 30; what scanning, exploration and navigation gained; scouts exploring, about 10 of 30; systems read in the three regions and on the roads to them.

