P4. Outposts, hubs and bases (G3, D4, rule F33). The Fleet Admiral chooses each site and allocates what it takes; Logistics builds, supplies and keeps it; Military guards it; Intel reads it first. (a) The first outpost stands 3 or 4 systems beyond our border (task 3). Read so far at that distance: Felis in the south (4 beyond: three ore belts, iron richness 54, the largest herds); in the east Intercrus (4; iron 45; four foreign pilots met), Atlas (3; iron 66), Botein (4; iron 68) and Acrux (4; iron 64, lithium); in the west Acrab (4; iron 62). An outpost: a fee of 100,000 cr and one Outpost Kit, a lawless system, a place without a station; it brings faction storage and a fuel bunker, costs no rent, serves members only, and stands with 12,000 hull and no shield (game/rules/09-factions.md, the base builder guide; not tried). (b) Intel reads the three regions and the roads to them; the divisions fill the systems on the way with routes that pay. (c) A large base is a station: a fee of 5,000,000 cr and a station core whose foundry alone takes 38,000,000 cr; the reserve gathers toward it and is never sold (rule F24).
  Goal: the first outpost founded, with its place and its cost; systems read in the three regions; routes that pay beyond our border.
P5. One fleet of 1,000 (G2): the faction's ladder, the Fleet Admiral's own. The Faction Lockbox stands at Node Beta Industrial Station since 2026-11-17 05:38 UTC: the faction's shared storage, 100,000 units; its rent, 69 cr a cycle or about 6,000 cr a day, comes from the treasury (44,862 cr), which she keeps filled. Next: 34 more ships join the faction (free; the limit reads 50, we are 16), the haulers first. Then the Hiring Board and the Recruitment Desk together: 375,000 cr, 275 steel plate, 125 circuit boards (51 boards held); the Fleet Admiral decides and pays each step, Logistics' flagship builds it (rule F11). The whole ladder: governance/empire.md.
  Goal: faction members of 50; the treasury's days of rent; circuit boards held against 125.
TURN (the tasks of now; each leaves the orders when it is done)
Task 1. Research your class (D3). Read the game for your own class: the official pages (https://www.spacemolt.com/docs/skills.md, the guide of your role, the help texts of your commands), the catalog (the tables `skills`, `hulls` and `items` through the tool hive with verb=sql; `skills.training` says how a skill is trained) and our own record (the tool fleet with view=levels; the table `ship_skills`). Write it down where every member finds it: your guidebook opens with the Class Card (rule F14), short and lean, before the Standing Plan: `### Ships`, how your class builds its ships (hull by tier, fit, style, in a few lines), and `### Skills`, one table of the skills of your class in the order they are levelled, the most important first (skill, why it matters, what trains it and by which of your routes, your division's level now, the next goal). The detail stays in your levelling guide. The Fleet Admiral checks every Class Card at her turn. Then fly it: most of your ships on work of your class; the work of another class is the exception, a little where it helps both (rule F32). Military: its drones train weapons and combat by hunting, guarding and the pirate test; they mine only when no herd and no post is open.
  Done when: your guidebook opens with the Class Card, each skill with how it is trained and its source; your report gives the ships of your division on work of its class before and after the turn.
Task 2. Hubs and outposts, 3 or 4 systems out (D4, rule F33): Suggi asks the group. Say in your report, under Suggestions: which system 3 or 4 jumps beyond our border would serve your division best as a hub for fuel and storage, what your ships would do from there, and what it would have to hold. P4 (a) names what is read at that distance. No outpost can stand in our own space (lawless systems only); The Experiment has a station, where a Faction Lockbox is the nearer storage if you need one.
  Done when: your report answers the three points for your division.
Task 3. The first outpost (P4 a): Intel and Mining say which of the systems 3 or 4 jumps out holds most for the fleet and is safest, with what was read there; Military plans its guard; Logistics plans its build as routes (the kit, its way there, the fuel for its bunker, the stock hauled in). The Fleet Admiral chooses the site after your reports and gives Logistics the credits and the faction's rights to build.
  Done when: your report names your part: a place with its readings, a guard plan, or a build plan with its routes.
Task 4. Two steps open since the last entry (rule F32; game/rules/08-combat.md). (a) Write `lift_to` into every standing route of yours beyond the border: the number of ships up to which fill.py may widen it by itself. (b) A ship pulled into a fight opens at its default stance, and `auto` opens an armed ship at fire; all of ours are armed. Set it by script as your class needs (spacemolt_battle default_stance): flee for a ship that is not there to fight (rule F2).
  Done when: your report names the routes that carry `lift_to` and the default stance you set, and for which ships.
EVERY ADMIRAL
Order: your mission stands in your COLLECTIVE.md, below the roster; your orders are your block here, and its Measure line is what your turn is judged by. You are an independent player of one fleet: inside your orders and the standing rules you decide alone and answer for the result in your report (the shared skill collective-play). Plan your division every turn in its plan file, as the shared skill fleet-play says under "Plan a Division"; sell what your ships store above the reserve on the sell routes Trade writes; build what you lack (the skill fleet-build, rule F21); answer the requests addressed to you; claim what you fill (admirals.log, rule F25). Beside your orders stands the goal of your class (G4): learn all of it the game holds, be in charge of all of it for the fleet, judge your own routes, levels and results before you report them, and name in your report one thing of your class you learned to do better and one you do not master yet.
Limits: the standing rules of the rulebook, section 12 (F1 to F33); above all the stance (F2), no word to another player (F4), no new pilot (F5), the wallet floor (F7), the money limit (F12), the frontier rule (F13), the reserve (F24) and where we build (F33). No pilot of ours by name in the repository: the logs and the route gate refuse it. A doubt about whose decision something is, is a request to the Fleet Admiral, never a guess; meanwhile every ship stays at work.
UNITY, Fleet Admiral (the command group)
Order: build and command the empire with the Admirals (G1): coordinate the plan, help each Admiral fulfil its role, build the plumbing they work through. Every turn: read the Admirals' reports and Feedback and fix what they name or open the door; answer every request before their next turn; keep the database, the catalog, the rules digest, the money figures, the tools and the station right; write the fleet report and lean orders (D7). Pay the faction's steps as they are held (P5), choose where each outpost and base stands and allocate what it takes, which Logistics then builds (P4, rules F31 and F33), check every guidebook's Class Card, decide what crosses divisions, and keep Suggi up to date. Bring to him only what is his: a new pilot, war on or a word to another player, a name off the enemy list. The command group levels, upgrades and earns like every division (rule F30).
Measure: requests to me still open when the Admirals' next turn starts, which should be none; the fleet's money per hour as a whole and its steady part; ships at work of 246; faction members of 50 and the treasury's days of rent; the site of the first outpost chosen and its build given to Logistics; Class Cards in five guidebooks; the orders no longer than the entry before, or the reason in its `Changed:` line.
ZERO, Mining (the miner- ships)
Order: sell ice only as far as each bid regains, spread over the stations that buy; every other miner gathers what the reserve is short of, on fields that hold, each with a floor: silicon ore and carbon ore where a field has them, then iron. Weigh a unit gathered against a unit bought with Trade, and say per ore what a ship gathers in an hour and what each field grows back. Fields beyond our space are yours to test and to widen (task 4 a). The stopgap of 2026-11-17 05:52 UTC is yours to reset: three lifts beyond the border (Schedar Belt to 45 ships, Ironhollow's two belts to 25 and 20) and the floors of four home fields lowered; a floor is there so that a field grows back. The iron belts you proposed for an outpost lie two jumps out, too close by rule F33: say with Intel which system 3 or 4 jumps out holds the fields Mining needs most (task 3).
Measure: what Mining brought the Collective: units stored for the reserve per hour and goods sold, beside its own income per hour (rule F31); miners at work, 60 of 60; silicon ore, carbon ore and iron ore held against what the reserve takes.
MERCURY, Trade (the trader- ships)
Order: find the trade that repeats, and sell the surplus: read it each turn (the tool stock with view=surplus), write or refresh a sell route for every good that has a bid, sized to what the bid takes, and say in queue.log which stores sell where. Where the bids are thin, test one standing sell order with one ship and one good. Buy the reserve's inputs where a unit bought costs less than a unit gathered, inside rule F12: carbon ore at about 4 cr as far as Logistics' boards use it; silicon ore only at a price you judge fair against a field still to be found. A trader without a paying trade sells surplus, carries or crafts for sale; it gathers ore only when none of that is open. A station beyond our space is tested with one ship and one unit. Price gaps between two stations (arbitrage) are yours to work as the books allow (D8): read both ends within the hour, one ship and one unit first. The hauling of fuel and supplies for our bases is Logistics'; what a base must buy, you buy.
Measure: income per hour from trade; surplus sold in credits, and what stays unsold with its reason; reserve inputs bought, in units per credit; traders at work, 40 of 40; the standing sell order tested once and its result read.
APOLLO, Logistics (the hauler- ships)
Order: you are the fleet's quartermaster and, from this entry, its base builder (D8, rule F16): you build our outposts, stations and facilities, make what they take, haul their fuel and supplies and keep them running. The Fleet Admiral chooses each site and allocates the credits and materials (rule F33); inside that the build is yours. (1) Research the builder's class (task 1): the official pages on stations, factions and base building, the help of spacemolt_facility, the tables `facilities` and `facility_materials`, the tool base_plan. Your guidebook says how an outpost is deployed and supplied, what a facility at a foreign station costs to keep, and what a station of our own draws every cycle. (2) Plan the first outpost as routes (task 3): the kit (two on offer at 74,010 cr at a player faction's station in lhs_1140, six jumps from Node Beta; making kits takes a facility of 600,000 cr), its way to the site, the fuel for its bunker, our stock hauled in. Deploy nothing yet: the site is chosen after this turn's reports, and the Fleet Admiral gives your flagship the faction's rights to build at her turn of 09:30 UTC, and the credits when the site stands. (3) Keep making: the 125 circuit boards and 275 steel plate of the Hiring Board and the Recruitment Desk (51 boards held), which your flagship builds at Node Beta when they are complete; steel by hand from stored iron. (4) Keep the reserve with the Fleet Admiral (the tool stock with view=reserve). A hauler without that work hauls or crafts; it gathers ore only when neither is open. Your haulers join the faction first (P5): the Lockbox and every outpost are theirs to fill.
Measure: the first outpost's build plan in your guidebook, with its routes written; circuit boards held against 125; inputs of the reserve with a known source; recipe steps tested; haulers at work, 40 of 40; later: bases built, and bases supplied without a gap.
APOPHIS, Military (the drone- ships)
Order: your drones fight, hunt and guard (task 1). Hunt as far as each herd regains, one hunter per herd (rule F9); guard where our ships gather and mine beyond the border (rule F16): since 2026-11-17 up to 45 ships of all divisions gather at Schedar Belt and up to 45 at Ironhollow's two belts: size the guard there, and say what a guard prevents and what it costs. What a kill yields and the reserve takes goes into storage where no bid stands for it. A division that tests work beyond our border asks you for a guard in requests.log: answer it yourself. Pirates are yours (rule F27): find them and write each reading down (observe.py add pirate <system id> ...); test first, one drone with a flee rule in the file against the weakest pirate read; from the test say what a group needs and what a kill earns, then gather it, three drones in one game fleet; all by scripts (rule F29). Your lasting objective: defend our ships, and later our outposts and stations; plan the guard of the first outpost before it is founded (task 3). Find why three of the first four grazer hunts did not reach their herds.
Measure: income per hour; inputs the reserve is short of stored per hour (surplus.py reserve names them; ore the fleet holds in plenty does not count); drones at work, 50 of 50, and of them on hunts, guards and tests; ships lost, which stays 0; what the combat skills gained; pirates: where they stand and what they are, the test's result, and what a group against them takes.
ORACLE, Intel (the scout- ships)
Order: a scout scans all the time, on templates of reads: the places our routes use are read twice in a turn, the order books the fleet sells to every hour, the oldest reading first; a scout gathers ore only when nothing the fleet plans on is stale. About ten scouts explore, about twenty stay where the fleet works, as you decide (rule F26). The explorers read first the three regions of D4 and the roads to them, the south and the east before the north, each read filed the same turn. Your scouts hunt as well (D8): where a herd lies on a scout's way it hunts, inside rule F9. For tasks 2 and 3, read the systems 3 or 4 jumps beyond our border that P4 (a) names, Felis and Intercrus first: the places without a station, the fields, the herds, who is there, where pirates stand, and the road to each; then say which serves best. Find a source for each input of the reserve that has none: a field of uranium ore first (every station bids 600 cr for it; requests ENT-194), then caustic sac and silicon ore. Write down where pirates stand, for Military (observe.py add pirate ...): your scouts look and leave (rule F27). Your scouts are light military where it serves: they escort, watch an outpost or a field and warn; the stance of rule F2 holds for them.
Measure: the age of the oldest reading the fleet plans on, at the end of the turn; places our routes use that were read twice; inputs of the reserve with a known source; scouts at work, 30 of 30; what scanning, exploration and navigation gained; scouts exploring, about 10 of 30; systems read in the three regions and on the roads to them.
